1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:01,845
Welcome, Aamer.

2
00:00:02,125 --> 00:00:10,275
This video walks you through your study packet for the Walmart Ads interview on Monday, October nineteenth, at three thirty in the afternoon.

3
00:00:10,555 --> 00:00:14,416
The role is Distinguished Data Scientist, Creative Intelligence.

4
00:00:14,696 --> 00:00:18,045
You already have the hard part, which is deep generative vision.

5
00:00:18,325 --> 00:00:22,485
This packet adds the ads business and the evaluation rigor they will probe.

6
00:00:24,117 --> 00:00:25,802
Here is the role in one breath.

7
00:00:26,082 --> 00:00:37,239
The job description asks for generative creative systems that produce images, copy and video that are high quality, on brand, compliant and performant, at advertiser scale.

8
00:00:37,519 --> 00:00:47,183
It asks for automated evaluation, in its words, models judging model output, calibrated to human and advertiser preference and monitored for drift.

9
00:00:47,463 --> 00:00:50,770
That line appears twice, so treat it as the heart of the role.

10
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:58,282
It also wants structured representations of creative content that targeting, recommendations and measurement can consume.

11
00:00:58,562 --> 00:01:04,493
Then automated composition and adaptation across formats, and shared infrastructure that compounds.

12
00:01:04,773 --> 00:01:09,893
On top sits leadership: turning ambiguous creative goals into a sequenced roadmap.

13
00:01:10,173 --> 00:01:19,111
In practice, they will test three things: scale with safety built in, reliable automated judges, and a clear link from creative to ad outcomes.

14
00:01:20,541 --> 00:01:22,354
The video has thirteen chapters.

15
00:01:22,634 --> 00:01:24,725
Chapters one and two decode the role.

16
00:01:25,005 --> 00:01:31,127
Chapters three and four teach the ads business and the serving stack, since this team is Ads Data Science.

17
00:01:31,407 --> 00:01:37,615
Chapters five through eight are the technical core: generation, the judge, creative signals, and video.

18
00:01:37,895 --> 00:01:44,018
Chapters nine and ten tie it together with the closed loop, the shared platform, and full system designs.

19
00:01:44,298 --> 00:01:50,122
Chapters eleven to thirteen cover your story, practice questions, and the last seventy-two hours.

20
00:01:50,402 --> 00:01:52,215
Use the written files alongside.

21
00:01:52,495 --> 00:01:59,471
The role start-here file is your map, the judge file is the most important read, and the cheat sheet is for Monday morning.

22
00:01:59,751 --> 00:02:01,885
Watch a chapter, then do its drills.

23
00:02:03,682 --> 00:02:07,544
This chapter reads the job description the way an interviewer does.

24
00:02:07,824 --> 00:02:10,981
Every question on Monday will map to one of six pillars.

25
00:02:11,261 --> 00:02:14,141
We will go pillar by pillar and name what they will test.

26
00:02:14,421 --> 00:02:16,597
Then we rank your gaps by interview risk.

27
00:02:16,877 --> 00:02:21,378
Last come the bridges: short lines that connect your real work to their needs.

28
00:02:23,008 --> 00:02:24,416
Here are the six pillars.

29
00:02:24,696 --> 00:02:33,998
Pillar one is generative creative systems: images, copy, video and other assets that are high quality, on brand, compliant and performant.

30
00:02:34,278 --> 00:02:38,715
Pillar two is automated evaluation, models judging model output.

31
00:02:38,995 --> 00:02:44,542
They name it twice, in the description and again in the requirements, so it is the heart of the role.

32
00:02:44,822 --> 00:02:53,526
Pillar three is structured representations of creative content, meaning attributes, categories and quality signals that other teams consume.

33
00:02:53,806 --> 00:02:59,672
Pillar four is automated composition and adaptation, so no one hand-produces every size.

34
00:02:59,952 --> 00:03:02,918
Pillar five is shared infrastructure that compounds.

35
00:03:03,198 --> 00:03:12,115
Finally, the leadership pillar: turning ambiguous, evolving goals into a sequenced roadmap, and explaining tradeoffs to senior business audiences.

36
00:03:13,550 --> 00:03:16,707
For pillar one, expect questions on product fidelity.

37
00:03:16,987 --> 00:03:22,897
The SKU, meaning the stock keeping unit, the exact product, cannot drift in shape, label or color.

38
00:03:23,177 --> 00:03:26,974
They will ask how safety and compliance are built in, not bolted on.

39
00:03:27,254 --> 00:03:34,614
They will ask whether generated creative moves CTR, the click-through rate, and what each asset costs in GPU time.

40
00:03:34,894 --> 00:03:37,070
For pillar two, expect a deep dive.

41
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:42,449
How do you design a rubric for a VLM, a vision language model, acting as judge?

42
00:03:42,729 --> 00:03:46,931
How do you calibrate its scores so they mean the probability a human approves?

43
00:03:47,211 --> 00:03:52,267
What biases do judges have, such as preferring the first option or longer answers?

44
00:03:52,547 --> 00:03:58,627
How do you detect drift, route uncertain cases to humans, and stop a generator from gaming the judge?

45
00:04:00,057 --> 00:04:04,516
For pillar three, they want a creative signal platform that other teams adopt.

46
00:04:04,796 --> 00:04:12,881
Expect questions on schema design, versioning, and who consumes the signals: ranking, targeting, recommendations and measurement.

47
00:04:13,161 --> 00:04:20,180
For pillar four, think smart cropping, outpainting a square shot to a tall format, re-layout, and keeping text legible.

48
00:04:20,460 --> 00:04:22,828
For pillar five, they want platform thinking.

49
00:04:23,108 --> 00:04:27,908
Can you build an eval harness and generation pipeline that every product reuses?

50
00:04:28,188 --> 00:04:31,004
The leadership pillar tests vision plus execution.

51
00:04:31,284 --> 00:04:33,054
What do you build first, and why?

52
00:04:33,334 --> 00:04:35,724
How do you move teams that do not report to you?

53
00:04:36,004 --> 00:04:42,958
Can you explain quality versus cost versus latency to a senior business leader in dollars and shopper impact?

54
00:04:44,390 --> 00:04:46,076
Quickly, the qualifications.

55
00:04:46,356 --> 00:04:50,942
You exceed the minimums with a master's degree and twenty-five years of experience.

56
00:04:51,222 --> 00:04:57,345
On preferred qualifications, your seven publications and five patents offset the missing PhD.

57
00:04:57,625 --> 00:05:00,313
Python, PyTorch and TensorFlow are solid.

58
00:05:00,593 --> 00:05:04,604
Spark and Scala are partial, so be honest and pivot to the modeling.

59
00:05:04,884 --> 00:05:13,438
One preferred line is easy points: wuh-cag two point two, level double A. wuh-cag means the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines.

60
00:05:13,718 --> 00:05:21,974
Alt text, contrast checks and captions for generated creative are natural judge dimensions, and we cover them in chapter eleven.

61
00:05:22,254 --> 00:05:23,790
The bigger message is this.

62
00:05:24,070 --> 00:05:30,726
Unlike general ads roles, this description is mostly generative computer vision with evaluation rigor.

63
00:05:31,006 --> 00:05:33,332
That is your core, so walk in confident.

64
00:05:34,765 --> 00:05:36,536
Now your gaps, ranked by risk.

65
00:05:36,816 --> 00:05:39,333
Number one is model-as-judge in production.

66
00:05:39,613 --> 00:05:45,117
You have built metrics, but not a production judge system with calibration and drift operations.

67
00:05:45,397 --> 00:05:48,619
Spend about five hours on the judge chapter and its drills.

68
00:05:48,899 --> 00:05:59,672
Number two is ads business context: how creative affects CTR, conversion rate and ROAS, which is return on ad spend, plus placements and creative testing.

69
00:05:59,952 --> 00:06:01,125
Give it three hours.

70
00:06:01,405 --> 00:06:04,221
Number three is video generation as a product.

71
00:06:04,501 --> 00:06:09,579
Your strength is frame synthesis and video evaluation, not text-to-video at scale.

72
00:06:09,859 --> 00:06:16,237
Fourth is safety, moderation, policy and intellectual property, which is its own requirement line.

73
00:06:16,517 --> 00:06:25,264
Then four smaller gaps: signals as a platform, grounded LLM copy, GPU cost per asset, and naming current models correctly.

74
00:06:26,693 --> 00:06:27,781
Now the bridges.

75
00:06:28,061 --> 00:06:29,555
Memorize the left column.

76
00:06:29,835 --> 00:06:33,760
Your identity-preserving face synthesis maps to product fidelity.

77
00:06:34,040 --> 00:06:39,757
Say: brand creative is identity-preserving generation where the identity is the SKU.

78
00:06:40,037 --> 00:06:44,752
Your inpainting work with gated and partial convolutions maps to adaptation.

79
00:06:45,032 --> 00:06:50,771
Say: outpainting a square product shot to nine by sixteen is the inpainting problem I have shipped.

80
00:06:51,051 --> 00:06:58,944
Your disocclusion detection produced pixel-accurate masks of where generation was needed, leaving verified pixels untouched.

81
00:06:59,224 --> 00:07:06,264
That is exactly the product-lock principle: lock what is real, generate only what is needed, verify the rest.

82
00:07:06,544 --> 00:07:10,661
And your spherical-harmonics lighting estimator answers a common failure.

83
00:07:10,941 --> 00:07:15,912
Say: most fake-looking composites fail on lighting, and I have built lighting estimators.

84
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:20,155
Four more bridges, and the first is your strongest.

85
00:07:20,435 --> 00:07:28,989
You built a reference-free temporal-quality metric for generated video, then published the negative result when a model gamed it as a training loss.

86
00:07:29,269 --> 00:07:36,971
Say: I have built a judge and watched a generator game it, so I keep held-out human anchors and never train against the deployed judge.

87
00:07:37,251 --> 00:07:40,984
Next, a patch discriminator is a learned artifact detector.

88
00:07:41,264 --> 00:07:46,875
It localizes artifacts, the same design as a production distortion detector with heatmaps.

89
00:07:47,155 --> 00:07:54,536
Your few-step consistency models speak to cost: distillation can cut GPU cost per asset several times over.

90
00:07:54,816 --> 00:07:58,784
Finally, your fused Golf Mode model beat per-sensor pipelines.

91
00:07:59,064 --> 00:08:04,248
That is the shared-platform argument: one model and signal store, not many silos.

92
00:08:06,043 --> 00:08:09,222
This chapter teaches you the business before the machine learning.

93
00:08:09,502 --> 00:08:14,899
You will learn who pays Walmart Connect, how they are billed, and which metrics they judge us by.

94
00:08:15,179 --> 00:08:19,382
Then you will see why creative quality is a rare lever that helps everyone.

95
00:08:21,027 --> 00:08:23,139
Start on the left with the advertisers.

96
00:08:23,419 --> 00:08:29,755
First-party suppliers, national brands like Procter and Gamble, sell to Walmart wholesale and spend the most.

97
00:08:30,035 --> 00:08:32,744
Marketplace sellers are long-tail and self-serve.

98
00:08:33,024 --> 00:08:36,843
Agencies buy display and CTV, meaning connected TV.

99
00:08:37,123 --> 00:08:43,821
Above them, budgets come from trade marketing first, and increasingly from brand budgets that used to fund TV.

100
00:08:44,101 --> 00:08:45,424
Thick arrows are money.

101
00:08:45,704 --> 00:08:48,989
Sponsored Products bills by CPC, cost per click.

102
00:08:49,269 --> 00:08:53,579
Display and CTV bill by CPM, cost per thousand impressions.

103
00:08:53,859 --> 00:08:57,869
Every buying path lands in one ad server, auction, and billing layer.

104
00:08:58,149 --> 00:09:07,109
It serves on-site pages, the open web through the DSP, a demand-side platform, Vizio screens, store screens, and the Sparky assistant.

105
00:09:07,389 --> 00:09:11,037
On-site inventory is owned, so it is nearly pure margin.

106
00:09:11,317 --> 00:09:12,640
Now the dotted arrows.

107
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:16,824
Impression logs join purchase logs, online and often in-store.

108
00:09:17,104 --> 00:09:20,155
Walmart observes the purchase instead of modeling it.

109
00:09:20,435 --> 00:09:25,021
That closed loop is the moat, because proven sales pull budget onto the platform.

110
00:09:26,451 --> 00:09:29,203
These five terms are the pricing vocabulary.

111
00:09:29,483 --> 00:09:33,152
CTR, click-through rate, is clicks divided by impressions.

112
00:09:33,432 --> 00:09:39,981
CVR, conversion rate, is conversions divided by clicks, usually within a fourteen-day window.

113
00:09:40,261 --> 00:09:42,821
CPC is spend divided by clicks.

114
00:09:43,101 --> 00:09:47,155
CPM is spend divided by impressions, times one thousand.

115
00:09:47,435 --> 00:09:51,595
The key one is eCPM, effective cost per thousand impressions.

116
00:09:51,875 --> 00:09:57,187
For a click-priced ad, it equals bid times predicted CTR, times one thousand.

117
00:09:57,467 --> 00:10:01,861
That puts every ad type on one revenue scale, so the auction can compare them.

118
00:10:03,291 --> 00:10:05,445
Advertisers judge us on outcomes.

119
00:10:05,725 --> 00:10:10,205
ROAS, return on ad spend, is attributed sales divided by spend.

120
00:10:10,485 --> 00:10:25,461
A ROAS of four means four dollars of sales per dollar spent. iROAS counts only incremental sales, the ones the ad caused, so it runs much lower than ROAS. ay-koss, advertising cost of sales, is one divided by ROAS.

121
00:10:25,741 --> 00:10:30,243
Finally, GMV, gross merchandise value, is Walmart's guardrail.

122
00:10:30,523 --> 00:10:37,755
No single ads launch may cut GMV per search, because organic conversion is the asset that ads monetize.

123
00:10:39,185 --> 00:10:42,214
This diagram is the central concept of the business.

124
00:10:42,494 --> 00:10:44,073
Three parties sit in tension.

125
00:10:44,353 --> 00:10:47,211
The shopper wants relevant results and a fast page.

126
00:10:47,491 --> 00:10:57,070
You measure shopper damage through organic signals: search conversion, GMV per search, and query reformulation, where shoppers rewrite a failed query.

127
00:10:57,350 --> 00:11:02,790
The advertiser wants sales and share of shelf, and sophisticated ones demand iROAS.

128
00:11:03,070 --> 00:11:07,742
Walmart wants ad revenue, retail GMV, and long-term shopper value.

129
00:11:08,022 --> 00:11:12,801
The double-headed edges are tensions: ad load, price and proof, and attention.

130
00:11:13,081 --> 00:11:20,846
Below sit the levers: ad load, relevance floor, reserve price, slot positions, frequency caps, and the creative quality bar.

131
00:11:21,126 --> 00:11:27,995
They meet in one blended score, eCPM plus alpha times relevance plus beta times shopper utility.

132
00:11:28,275 --> 00:11:30,302
Long-term experiments set those weights.

133
00:11:30,582 --> 00:11:31,883
Here is the key idea.

134
00:11:32,163 --> 00:11:34,787
Most levers just move value between parties.

135
00:11:35,067 --> 00:11:40,806
Creative quality raises CTR at the same relevance and ad load, so it helps all three.

136
00:11:41,086 --> 00:11:44,307
In the interview, call it a GMV-safe revenue lever.

137
00:11:45,737 --> 00:11:48,830
Here is the product landscape, grouped by inventory.

138
00:11:49,110 --> 00:11:53,675
First, owned on-site inventory, the highest margin and highest intent.

139
00:11:53,955 --> 00:12:01,358
Sponsored Products is a tile that looks like an organic result, so its creative is mostly the catalog image and title.

140
00:12:01,638 --> 00:12:05,905
Sponsored Brands is a banner with logo, headline, and several products.

141
00:12:06,185 --> 00:12:09,235
That is where generated creative first clearly matters.

142
00:12:09,515 --> 00:12:12,097
Sponsored Videos are video units in search.

143
00:12:12,377 --> 00:12:16,665
These three are typically CPC, while on-site display is CPM.

144
00:12:16,945 --> 00:12:18,075
Next, off-site.

145
00:12:18,355 --> 00:12:24,542
The Walmart DSP buys open-web impressions with first-party audiences, in first-price auctions.

146
00:12:24,822 --> 00:12:32,182
CTV through Vizio, publicly reported as a two point three billion dollar acquisition, is also CPM.

147
00:12:32,462 --> 00:12:38,841
Then come in-store screens, and sponsored placements inside Sparky, an emerging conversational format.

148
00:12:39,121 --> 00:12:46,033
Finally the tools: Ad Center, the Marty advertiser agent, the Automated Creative Generator, and measurement.

149
00:12:46,313 --> 00:12:47,785
Notice the dotted arrows.

150
00:12:48,065 --> 00:12:50,689
One creative generator feeds many products.

151
00:12:50,969 --> 00:12:53,486
That is your argument for one shared platform.

152
00:12:54,916 --> 00:12:57,540
Read this one left to right as the shopper's journey.

153
00:12:57,820 --> 00:12:59,164
Awareness comes first.

154
00:12:59,444 --> 00:13:04,030
CTV, off-site video, and display reach shoppers before they have intent.

155
00:13:04,310 --> 00:13:09,964
You buy reach at CPM and measure brand lift, and with closed-loop data, purchase lift too.

156
00:13:10,244 --> 00:13:19,076
Consideration means Sponsored Brands, Sponsored Videos, and on-site display, judged by CTR, add-to-cart, and new-to-brand engagement.

157
00:13:19,356 --> 00:13:25,606
Conversion is Sponsored Products on high-intent queries, priced by CPC and judged by ROAS.

158
00:13:25,886 --> 00:13:31,646
Loyalty covers retargeting and reorder prompts, measured by repeat rate and lifetime value.

159
00:13:31,926 --> 00:13:36,577
The dotted arrow back to awareness means loyal buyers seed lookalike audiences.

160
00:13:36,857 --> 00:13:42,489
Underneath sits a shared purchase ledger, so every stage is measured against the same transactions.

161
00:13:42,769 --> 00:13:44,860
Most media companies cannot do that.

162
00:13:45,140 --> 00:13:47,145
The last box matters most for you.

163
00:13:47,425 --> 00:13:56,748
Creative changes by stage: emotional story for CTV, brand plus products for Sponsored Brands, and product clarity for Sponsored Products.

164
00:13:57,028 --> 00:14:00,718
So placement and objective are first-class generator inputs.

165
00:14:02,166 --> 00:14:05,323
Now the campaign lifecycle, drawn as a state machine.

166
00:14:05,603 --> 00:14:07,160
A campaign starts in Draft.

167
00:14:07,440 --> 00:14:15,056
The advertiser, or Marty on their behalf, sets objective, budget, targeting, and SKUs, meaning the specific products.

168
00:14:15,336 --> 00:14:16,872
Next is Creative build.

169
00:14:17,152 --> 00:14:20,438
For Sponsored Products that is mostly the catalog listing.

170
00:14:20,718 --> 00:14:24,856
For brands, video, and display, assets are uploaded or generated.

171
00:14:25,136 --> 00:14:28,934
Then comes policy and quality review, often human-heavy today.

172
00:14:29,214 --> 00:14:31,944
A failure goes to Needs changes and loops back.

173
00:14:32,224 --> 00:14:38,091
With generative tools, that loop becomes regenerate with the critique, not a week with an agency.

174
00:14:38,371 --> 00:14:40,867
Approved campaigns become Scheduled or Live.

175
00:14:41,147 --> 00:14:47,270
Optimizing is separate because autobidding needs conversions and creative tests need impressions.

176
00:14:47,550 --> 00:14:51,752
Paused has retail reasons, like budget exhaustion or out of stock.

177
00:14:52,032 --> 00:14:56,662
Reporting waits for the attribution window to close, then feeds a cloned relaunch.

178
00:14:56,942 --> 00:14:59,288
Creative intelligence changes two states.

179
00:14:59,568 --> 00:15:03,323
Automated evaluation, chapter six, shrinks review latency.

180
00:15:03,603 --> 00:15:07,571
Creative insights at reporting improve the next campaign's ROAS.

181
00:15:09,351 --> 00:15:15,687
This chapter follows one search ad request from the query to the winning ad, then through training and measurement.

182
00:15:15,967 --> 00:15:17,481
You already know the modeling.

183
00:15:17,761 --> 00:15:22,519
The new parts are economic: calibrated probabilities, auctions, and budgets.

184
00:15:22,799 --> 00:15:25,657
At every stage, watch for where creative enters.

185
00:15:27,292 --> 00:15:29,298
The shopper searches for paper towels.

186
00:15:29,578 --> 00:15:36,639
The front end calls organic search and the ad server in parallel, so ads gets roughly thirty to fifty milliseconds.

187
00:15:36,919 --> 00:15:41,676
Query understanding spell-corrects, classifies the category, and embeds the query.

188
00:15:41,956 --> 00:15:49,700
Retrieval pulls candidates three ways: lexical keyword match, semantic two-tower nearest neighbors, and behavioral co-buy.

189
00:15:49,980 --> 00:15:55,612
The budget service drops campaigns with no money left, leaving one to five thousand candidates.

190
00:15:55,892 --> 00:15:59,796
One batched feature fetch gets user, ad, and creative features.

191
00:16:00,076 --> 00:16:02,316
That fetch is often the real bottleneck.

192
00:16:02,596 --> 00:16:09,338
The ranker returns predicted CTR, or click-through rate, predicted CVR, or conversion rate, and relevance.

193
00:16:09,618 --> 00:16:12,263
The auction picks winners, slots, and prices.

194
00:16:12,543 --> 00:16:15,700
A page blender merges ads into organic slots.

195
00:16:15,980 --> 00:16:20,866
Impressions count only when viewable, and serve-time features are logged for training.

196
00:16:21,146 --> 00:16:27,162
The ad server chooses which creative, but a CDN, a content delivery network, serves the pixels.

197
00:16:27,442 --> 00:16:31,943
So expensive generation stays offline and never touches serving latency.

198
00:16:33,376 --> 00:16:36,170
Here is the same request as a funnel of models.

199
00:16:36,450 --> 00:16:41,848
At the top sits the ad corpus, millions of item, campaign, and creative combinations.

200
00:16:42,128 --> 00:16:46,160
No heavy model can score them all at a hundred thousand queries per second.

201
00:16:46,440 --> 00:16:51,538
Retrieval takes about ten milliseconds and returns one to five thousand candidates.

202
00:16:51,818 --> 00:16:59,200
Eligibility filters remove anything without budget, out of stock at the shopper's store, mistargeted, or against policy.

203
00:16:59,480 --> 00:17:03,149
A light pre-ranker cuts to a few hundred in about five milliseconds.

204
00:17:03,429 --> 00:17:07,013
It learns to imitate the ranker: the dotted distillation arrow.

205
00:17:07,293 --> 00:17:12,584
The heavy ranker scores those in ten to fifteen milliseconds, and creative features enter here.

206
00:17:12,864 --> 00:17:17,877
The auction stage applies floors, diversity, and pricing, leaving two to eight winners.

207
00:17:18,157 --> 00:17:23,042
The arrow back measures recall at K per stage, revealing which stage drops good ads.

208
00:17:23,322 --> 00:17:24,901
Here is the creative point.

209
00:17:25,181 --> 00:17:32,050
Generated variants multiply candidates, so make creative selection a late, cheap stage after item ranking.

210
00:17:33,501 --> 00:17:35,762
Now inside the ranker, top to bottom.

211
00:17:36,042 --> 00:17:40,906
Sparse IDs go into sharded embedding tables that reach hundreds of gigabytes.

212
00:17:41,186 --> 00:17:49,122
The behavior sequence uses target attention, where the candidate item is the query, as in DIN, the deep interest network.

213
00:17:49,402 --> 00:17:53,434
Dense features like price and historical CTR are normalized.

214
00:17:53,714 --> 00:18:01,991
Then the creative hook: precomputed multimodal embeddings of the product image, title, and creative image, CLIP or sig-lip style.

215
00:18:02,271 --> 00:18:08,266
Everything meets in DCN-v2, a deep and cross network, plus a multilayer perceptron.

216
00:18:08,546 --> 00:18:15,394
Shared experts with per-task gates, M-M-O-E, multi-gate mixture of experts, let tasks share and diverge.

217
00:18:15,674 --> 00:18:19,535
Heads predict CTR, CVR, add-to-cart, and relevance.

218
00:18:19,815 --> 00:18:28,989
The CVR head is E-S-M-M style, the entire space multi-task model, trained on all impressions to remove sample selection bias.

219
00:18:29,269 --> 00:18:33,173
A shallow position tower absorbs slot bias in training only.

220
00:18:33,453 --> 00:18:35,415
Last comes per-segment calibration.

221
00:18:35,695 --> 00:18:43,546
In the interview, say creative embeddings give a new generated creative a sensible predicted CTR before any impressions.

222
00:18:44,976 --> 00:18:50,672
The auction multiplies predicted CTR by the bid, and pacing treats it as a probability.

223
00:18:50,952 --> 00:18:56,648
Great A-U-C, a pure ranking metric, with twenty percent over-prediction still overcharges.

224
00:18:56,928 --> 00:19:01,024
Monitor the calibration ratio, predicted over actual, per segment.

225
00:19:01,304 --> 00:19:07,426
Negative downsampling, keeping a fraction w of negatives, inflates the raw output, p prime.

226
00:19:07,706 --> 00:19:14,149
Correct it: p equals p prime, divided by p prime plus one minus p prime over w.

227
00:19:14,429 --> 00:19:17,949
At ten percent, a raw one half becomes about nine percent.

228
00:19:18,229 --> 00:19:22,325
Segment new ads too, since generated creatives miscalibrate first.

229
00:19:23,767 --> 00:19:24,813
Now the auction.

230
00:19:25,093 --> 00:19:30,063
Candidates arrive with a bid, a calibrated predicted CTR, and relevance.

231
00:19:30,343 --> 00:19:32,477
The first gate is the relevance floor.

232
00:19:32,757 --> 00:19:37,535
No amount of money buys an irrelevant ad onto the page, which protects the shopper.

233
00:19:37,815 --> 00:19:45,623
Next, compute eCPM, revenue per thousand impressions: bid times predicted CTR times one thousand.

234
00:19:45,903 --> 00:19:51,941
The second gate is the reserve price, a minimum eCPM that protects prices in thin auctions.

235
00:19:52,221 --> 00:19:54,375
It can be learned per query or category.

236
00:19:54,655 --> 00:20:01,823
Survivors are sorted by eCPM, deduplicated, diversified, and allocated up to the ad load cap.

237
00:20:02,103 --> 00:20:05,239
Pricing is GSP, generalized second price.

238
00:20:05,519 --> 00:20:12,069
Each winner pays the minimum needed to keep its slot, clamped between reserve and its own bid, and only on a click.

239
00:20:12,349 --> 00:20:17,106
Off-site demand-side platform auctions are first price, so bidders shade bids.

240
00:20:17,386 --> 00:20:22,165
Every participant is logged, including losers, for counterfactual analysis.

241
00:20:22,445 --> 00:20:27,671
The creative tie-in: higher predicted CTR wins a better slot at a lower price.

242
00:20:29,101 --> 00:20:30,552
Memorize this example.

243
00:20:30,832 --> 00:20:37,872
Slot i pays the next ad's score, bid times predicted CTR, divided by its own predicted CTR.

244
00:20:38,152 --> 00:20:42,333
A bids one dollar at point zero five, scoring point zero five.

245
00:20:42,613 --> 00:20:46,645
B bids two dollars at point zero two, scoring point zero four.

246
00:20:46,925 --> 00:20:51,192
C bids fifty cents at point zero six, scoring point zero three.

247
00:20:51,472 --> 00:20:55,269
A pays point zero four over point zero five, eighty cents.

248
00:20:55,549 --> 00:20:59,475
B pays point zero three over point zero two, a dollar fifty.

249
00:20:59,755 --> 00:21:01,973
A wins the top slot with the lowest bid.

250
00:21:02,253 --> 00:21:04,237
Better creative is cheaper media.

251
00:21:05,685 --> 00:21:12,533
Most advertisers set a goal, a budget, and maybe a target ROAS, return on ad spend, not per-keyword bids.

252
00:21:12,813 --> 00:21:19,063
A value model estimates each opportunity as predicted CVR times expected order value.

253
00:21:19,343 --> 00:21:24,549
Calibration errors in that value model become direct overspending or underspending.

254
00:21:24,829 --> 00:21:27,154
The bid is that value divided by lambda.

255
00:21:27,434 --> 00:21:31,402
Bids enter auctions, producing wins, clicks, spend, and conversions.

256
00:21:31,682 --> 00:21:39,149
A spend tracker compares actual spend against a target curve shaped by expected traffic, not a straight line through the day.

257
00:21:39,429 --> 00:21:41,242
The controller then updates lambda.

258
00:21:41,522 --> 00:21:44,103
Overspending raises lambda, so bids fall.

259
00:21:44,383 --> 00:21:46,709
Underspending lowers it, so bids rise.

260
00:21:46,989 --> 00:21:52,087
Lambda refreshes every few minutes, while delayed conversions retrain the value model.

261
00:21:52,367 --> 00:21:57,423
Throttling, joining each auction with some probability, is a simpler but cruder lever.

262
00:21:57,703 --> 00:21:59,602
Here is the creative interaction.

263
00:21:59,882 --> 00:22:03,999
If a new variant raises CTR, spend jumps and the pacer reacts.

264
00:22:04,279 --> 00:22:11,042
So measure creative lift with budget-split or advertiser-level randomization, never naive impression splits.

265
00:22:12,476 --> 00:22:16,530
The advertiser maximizes total value, the sum of x times v.

266
00:22:16,810 --> 00:22:20,308
Here x says whether you win auction i, and v is its value.

267
00:22:20,588 --> 00:22:24,428
Total cost, the sum of x times c, must stay within budget B.

268
00:22:24,708 --> 00:22:30,682
The Lagrangian dual gives the optimal bid: v divided by lambda, the shadow price of budget.

269
00:22:30,962 --> 00:22:33,778
Scarce budget means high lambda and lower bids.

270
00:22:34,058 --> 00:22:39,284
Each step, lambda moves by a learning rate, eta, times the spend error, floored at zero.

271
00:22:39,564 --> 00:22:44,471
Ship this dual-gradient controller first, then test reinforcement learning against it.

272
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:47,671
Training data starts at serving.

273
00:22:47,951 --> 00:22:54,970
The ad server logs exactly the features the model saw, because recomputing them later causes training-serving skew.

274
00:22:55,250 --> 00:23:01,500
Three Kafka streams carry impressions with auctions, clicks, and orders, including linked store transactions.

275
00:23:01,780 --> 00:23:04,767
A stream join by impression ID builds labels.

276
00:23:05,047 --> 00:23:09,164
Clicks join in seconds, but orders can take the full attribution window.

277
00:23:09,444 --> 00:23:12,836
So you either wait, or train now with a delayed-feedback model.

278
00:23:13,116 --> 00:23:21,927
Batch pipelines fill an offline feature store with point-in-time correct joins, and an online store for fast lookup, from the same definitions.

279
00:23:22,207 --> 00:23:25,599
Training is a daily full retrain plus hourly increments.

280
00:23:25,879 --> 00:23:30,295
Validation checks A-U-C, log loss, and calibration by segment.

281
00:23:30,575 --> 00:23:37,210
Then a model registry, shadow, canary, and an online experiment with gross merchandise value guardrails.

282
00:23:37,490 --> 00:23:38,898
The dotted loop matters.

283
00:23:39,178 --> 00:23:46,047
The model decides what is shown, which decides the next data, so generated creatives need exploration traffic.

284
00:23:46,327 --> 00:23:49,954
And log creative ID and creative attributes from day one.

285
00:23:51,400 --> 00:23:53,832
Exposure data comes from four sources.

286
00:23:54,112 --> 00:24:04,310
They are on-site impressions and clicks, off-site display impressions, connected TV exposures via Vizio, and store screen plays known only by store and time.

287
00:24:04,590 --> 00:24:09,454
Purchase data is online orders plus in-store transactions linked to accounts.

288
00:24:09,734 --> 00:24:15,088
Identity resolution joins them at account or household level, under privacy controls.

289
00:24:15,368 --> 00:24:20,211
Attribution credits sales by rules like last touch in a fourteen-day window.

290
00:24:20,491 --> 00:24:23,456
But attribution only says which ads were near the sale.

291
00:24:23,736 --> 00:24:25,955
Incrementality says which ads caused it.

292
00:24:26,235 --> 00:24:29,542
Randomized holdouts withhold ads from a control group.

293
00:24:29,822 --> 00:24:34,280
Ghost ads log, in control, which ad would have won, so you compare like with like.

294
00:24:34,560 --> 00:24:38,080
Store screens and connected TV need geo experiments.

295
00:24:38,360 --> 00:24:46,467
Reporting shows ROAS, iROAS, meaning incremental ROAS, and halo, with MRC, the Media Rating Council, auditing the counts.

296
00:24:46,747 --> 00:24:51,611
Search incrementality reporting was publicly launched in 2025.

297
00:24:51,891 --> 00:24:59,230
For creative, optimize incremental conversions per dollar, because optimizing CTR alone finds clickbait.

298
00:25:01,009 --> 00:25:04,273
Welcome to chapter five, the technical core of this job.

299
00:25:04,553 --> 00:25:12,084
The role asks for systems that produce on-brand, compliant images, copy and composed ads without manual production.

300
00:25:12,364 --> 00:25:16,609
This is where your diffusion, inpainting and relighting work pays off most.

301
00:25:16,889 --> 00:25:21,753
By the end, you should be able to draw this pipeline from memory and defend every choice.

302
00:25:23,383 --> 00:25:27,735
First, place creative inside the ads system, using Diagram thirteen.

303
00:25:28,015 --> 00:25:30,255
On the left are three sources of creative.

304
00:25:30,535 --> 00:25:36,189
The catalog comes first, and for Sponsored Products the creative is simply the catalog listing.

305
00:25:36,469 --> 00:25:41,375
Next come advertiser uploads, and then generated creatives, your team's output.

306
00:25:41,655 --> 00:25:45,389
All three feed the creative asset store in the middle, which is the hub.

307
00:25:45,669 --> 00:25:50,789
Every asset carries lineage, meaning who or what made it, plus extracted attributes.

308
00:25:51,069 --> 00:25:53,842
On the right are five consumers of those assets.

309
00:25:54,122 --> 00:25:58,069
Ad review sees assets first, and only approved ones reach ranking.

310
00:25:58,349 --> 00:26:03,682
Ranking uses creative features to predict click-through rate and to pick which creative to show.

311
00:26:03,962 --> 00:26:11,471
Rendering produces each format, targeting matches attributes to context, and measurement reports performance by attribute.

312
00:26:11,751 --> 00:26:19,090
Thick arrows carry the shopper's response into logs keyed by creative ID, and dotted arrows loop back into generation.

313
00:26:19,370 --> 00:26:25,429
Your line: creative should be a structured, versioned, queryable object, not a JPEG URL.

314
00:26:26,858 --> 00:26:33,365
Here's the key mental model: an ad is not one image, but a stack of layers, each with its own source of truth.

315
00:26:33,645 --> 00:26:37,357
The product cutout comes from the catalog, and its pixels are locked.

316
00:26:37,637 --> 00:26:41,733
The scene is generated, and it is the only truly open canvas.

317
00:26:42,013 --> 00:26:47,602
Headlines come from a large language model, or LLM, but only from verified facts.

318
00:26:47,882 --> 00:26:53,173
The call to action, or CTA, comes from a fixed list, and the logo is a vector file.

319
00:26:53,453 --> 00:26:59,682
Price, rating and badges are live data, bound at render or serve time, so they never go stale.

320
00:26:59,962 --> 00:27:07,109
In the interview, say it plainly: price, logo, rating and legal text are data, and data is never generated.

321
00:27:08,538 --> 00:27:12,592
You know these models deeply, so focus on the decisions they drive.

322
00:27:12,872 --> 00:27:19,805
In latent diffusion, the variational autoencoder, or V-A-E, can blur packaging text on a round trip alone.

323
00:27:20,085 --> 00:27:23,221
So product pixels should never pass through the generator.

324
00:27:23,501 --> 00:27:31,416
Diffusion transformers, or D-I-T models, trained with flow matching, handle many aspect ratios natively with fewer steps.

325
00:27:31,696 --> 00:27:36,389
Classifier-free guidance, or CFG, samples a sharpened distribution.

326
00:27:36,669 --> 00:27:43,218
High guidance therefore oversaturates and kills diversity, so rescale it or guide only at mid-noise.

327
00:27:43,498 --> 00:27:51,840
Few-step distillation turns sixty network passes into four, about fifteen times less denoising, your consistency-model territory.

328
00:27:52,120 --> 00:28:02,082
On adapters, LoRA learns a brand style, IP-Adapter borrows style from a reference image without training, and ControlNet supplies depth and layout.

329
00:28:02,362 --> 00:28:07,653
All three give resemblance, never pixel-exact products, so the product gets composited.

330
00:28:09,082 --> 00:28:14,501
Now the centerpiece, Diagram fourteen, starting with the advertiser inputs at the top.

331
00:28:14,781 --> 00:28:19,026
The SKU, or stock keeping unit, anchors everything to a real product.

332
00:28:19,306 --> 00:28:25,920
The brand kit carries logos, fonts, colors and hard rules, while goal and placements set style and sizes.

333
00:28:26,200 --> 00:28:32,877
Catalog fetch pulls hero images, attributes and approved claims, the source of truth for pixels and words.

334
00:28:33,157 --> 00:28:40,240
On the visual branch, segmentation and matting cut out the product with a soft alpha edge, and those pixels are now locked.

335
00:28:40,520 --> 00:28:48,008
Scene generation inpaints around that mask, with depth or edge control for perspective and a style adapter for brand look.

336
00:28:48,288 --> 00:28:51,210
Harmonization then matches the light and adds shadows.

337
00:28:51,490 --> 00:28:58,317
On the copy branch, the LLM writes structured JSON from a fact sheet, and every claim must cite a fact ID.

338
00:28:58,597 --> 00:29:05,637
The claim checker runs entailment against those facts, exact-matches numbers, and blocks words like best or cures.

339
00:29:05,917 --> 00:29:10,440
Defend two principles: product pixels are sacred, and words are grounded.

340
00:29:11,874 --> 00:29:18,893
Product fidelity is the make-or-break problem, because a wrong variant or garbled label is a false advertisement.

341
00:29:19,173 --> 00:29:26,831
Architecture handles most of it: the generator only paints outside the mask, and you re-paste the original pixels afterwards.

342
00:29:27,111 --> 00:29:31,677
The hard part is making the paste look real, and that's your spherical-harmonics strength.

343
00:29:31,957 --> 00:29:35,498
Estimate the scene light as nine coefficients per color channel.

344
00:29:35,778 --> 00:29:40,045
Then multiply the product by the ratio of scene shading to source shading.

345
00:29:40,325 --> 00:29:45,125
That field is smooth, so it cannot damage text or logo edges by construction.

346
00:29:45,405 --> 00:29:50,695
Bound it near half a stop, check brand-color drift, and add shadows from a fitted ground plane.

347
00:29:50,975 --> 00:29:58,719
Then verify with pixel lock, optical character recognition diffs, Dino v two similarity, and count and scale checks.

348
00:30:00,166 --> 00:30:04,240
Now the lower half of the same diagram, where everything gets composed.

349
00:30:04,520 --> 00:30:09,000
The branches meet at the layout and composition engine, which is deterministic.

350
00:30:09,280 --> 00:30:14,891
Templates plus a constraint solver place the logo, headline, CTA and late-bound price.

351
00:30:15,171 --> 00:30:18,115
Text is rendered as vector type in the brand font.

352
00:30:18,395 --> 00:30:23,600
So spelling is exact, prices stay fresh, and localization is just a string swap.

353
00:30:23,880 --> 00:30:30,515
In per-placement adaptation, sizes run from eight to one wide to one to four tall, so no image fits all.

354
00:30:30,795 --> 00:30:35,296
If the aspect change is small, smart crop, keeping the product fully inside.

355
00:30:35,576 --> 00:30:40,995
If the scene is extendable, outpaint, which is your inpainting work with the mask inverted.

356
00:30:41,275 --> 00:30:45,990
For a seven twenty-eight by ninety leaderboard, re-layout onto a brand-color plate.

357
00:30:46,270 --> 00:30:49,854
Generate scenes once per aspect family, not per placement.

358
00:30:50,134 --> 00:30:58,902
Finally, the router sends confident passes to the advertiser, uncertain items to humans, and failures back to regenerate with the critique.

359
00:31:00,332 --> 00:31:04,535
Accessibility is a preferred qualification that most candidates skip.

360
00:31:04,815 --> 00:31:09,487
Since the compositor knows every glyph and color, contrast becomes exact math.

361
00:31:09,767 --> 00:31:18,428
First, take each sRGB channel as c, the eight-bit value divided by two fifty-five, and linearize it with the curve on line one.

362
00:31:18,708 --> 00:31:24,831
Second, relative luminance L weights the linear red, green and blue values, with green weighted most.

363
00:31:25,111 --> 00:31:44,183
Third, the contrast ratio CR is the lighter luminance L one plus point zero five, divided by the darker L two plus point zero five. wuh-cag, the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, at level double-A, needs four point five to one for body text and three to one for large text.

364
00:31:44,463 --> 00:31:48,303
White on a typical red scores about four point two, so it fails.

365
00:31:49,749 --> 00:31:53,248
Interviewers probe two more areas, starting with brand at scale.

366
00:31:53,528 --> 00:31:57,773
You can't maintain a LoRA for each of thousands of brands, so tier it.

367
00:31:58,053 --> 00:32:03,642
The long tail gets reference-image conditioning and palette prompts, at near-zero cost per brand.

368
00:32:03,922 --> 00:32:08,637
Top spenders get a LoRA only when an A B test shows the lift pays off.

369
00:32:08,917 --> 00:32:13,589
Colors that matter are rendered, then checked with Delta E in C-I-E lab space.

370
00:32:13,869 --> 00:32:18,989
The second area is safety, where prevention is soft and detection decides what ships.

371
00:32:19,269 --> 00:32:26,458
Classifiers check for explicit content, minors, competitor logos, celebrity likeness and stray scene text.

372
00:32:26,738 --> 00:32:31,709
A category policy engine covers regulated goods, defaulting to the strictest rule.

373
00:32:31,989 --> 00:32:38,474
Every asset carries a C-two-P-A provenance manifest plus a watermark, since ad pipelines strip metadata.

374
00:32:39,916 --> 00:32:43,137
Cost math shows judgment, so state your assumptions first.

375
00:32:43,417 --> 00:32:48,260
Assume a GPU at three dollars an hour, and fifty milliseconds per denoising step.

376
00:32:48,540 --> 00:32:53,105
Thirty steps plus decode and gates is two GPU-seconds per candidate.

377
00:32:53,385 --> 00:33:00,148
Generate four candidates, then divide by sixty percent utilization and an eighty-five percent pass rate.

378
00:33:00,428 --> 00:33:05,078
That's about sixteen GPU-seconds, or one point three cents per approved scene.

379
00:33:05,358 --> 00:33:08,281
Four distilled steps land near four tenths of a cent.

380
00:33:08,561 --> 00:33:15,921
Denoising fell fifteen times, but total cost fell only about three times, because gates and decoding now dominate.

381
00:33:16,201 --> 00:33:23,604
At half a million SKUs, review and quality failures cost more than GPUs, so invest in gate accuracy first.

382
00:33:25,391 --> 00:33:28,249
Chapter six, and the most important one for this role.

383
00:33:28,529 --> 00:33:39,815
The job description names it twice: models judging model output, calibrated against human and advertiser preference, with drift detection and reliability at production scale.

384
00:33:40,095 --> 00:33:42,100
Expect at least one deep dive here.

385
00:33:42,380 --> 00:33:46,604
It is also where your published negative result becomes your strongest story.

386
00:33:48,249 --> 00:33:50,766
I'd build a judge stack, not a single judge.

387
00:33:51,046 --> 00:33:57,894
Cheap deterministic checks, then specialists, then a VLM, a vision-language model, scoring against a rubric.

388
00:33:58,174 --> 00:34:01,737
Every score is calibrated to the probability a human approves.

389
00:34:02,017 --> 00:34:05,900
A risk-based router auto-approves only above target precision.

390
00:34:06,180 --> 00:34:10,745
I monitor drift, and I never let the generator optimize against the gatekeeper.

391
00:34:11,025 --> 00:34:15,676
Open any evaluation question with this, then let them pick a box to drill into.

392
00:34:17,124 --> 00:34:18,169
Start with why.

393
00:34:18,449 --> 00:34:19,879
Generation is cheap.

394
00:34:20,159 --> 00:34:21,503
Human review is not.

395
00:34:21,783 --> 00:34:30,252
Picture thousands of brands, times millions of SKUs, the individual catalog products, times dozens of placements and variants.

396
00:34:30,532 --> 00:34:35,225
Creative volume grows combinatorially, while review capacity grows linearly.

397
00:34:35,505 --> 00:34:36,935
So here's the key idea.

398
00:34:37,215 --> 00:34:43,977
Your judge's precision at the auto-approve threshold decides how much generated creative you can actually ship.

399
00:34:44,257 --> 00:34:46,071
Next, trust is asymmetric.

400
00:34:46,351 --> 00:34:53,881
One hallucinated claim or distorted logo shown to a national brand costs more than a thousand good images earn.

401
00:34:54,161 --> 00:34:57,959
That asymmetry, not raw accuracy, should set your operating point.

402
00:34:58,239 --> 00:35:04,233
Finally, evaluation plays three roles at once: gatekeeper, coach for the generator, and scoreboard.

403
00:35:04,513 --> 00:35:05,665
Keep them separate.

404
00:35:05,945 --> 00:35:07,503
We return to why at the end.

405
00:35:08,932 --> 00:35:12,196
Before building any judge, write a failure-mode catalog.

406
00:35:12,476 --> 00:35:16,913
Every dimension gets an owner, a detector, a golden set and a target.

407
00:35:17,193 --> 00:35:19,220
On the left are the critical dimensions.

408
00:35:19,500 --> 00:35:22,977
Product fidelity, like a morphed product or a garbled label.

409
00:35:23,257 --> 00:35:25,604
Hallucinated claims and wrong prices.

410
00:35:25,884 --> 00:35:27,185
Policy and safety.

411
00:35:27,465 --> 00:35:28,831
Intellectual property.

412
00:35:29,111 --> 00:35:30,433
And severe distortion.

413
00:35:30,713 --> 00:35:37,284
On the right are the softer ones: appeal, scene fit, brand style, and predicted click-through rate, or CTR.

414
00:35:37,564 --> 00:35:39,505
Here's the Distinguished-level point.

415
00:35:39,785 --> 00:35:43,199
Quality is multi-dimensional, with different loss functions.

416
00:35:43,479 --> 00:35:46,209
Gates are vetoes, tuned for recall of failures.

417
00:35:46,489 --> 00:35:49,497
Objectives are ranked, optimized and A B tested.

418
00:35:49,777 --> 00:35:52,295
Never collapse them into one score for gating.

419
00:35:52,575 --> 00:35:56,116
A beautiful image that misrepresents the product must still fail.

420
00:35:57,557 --> 00:36:00,800
Here is the judge stack, Diagram fifteen in your packet.

421
00:36:01,080 --> 00:36:02,317
Read it from the top.

422
00:36:02,597 --> 00:36:08,165
The candidate creative arrives with its SKU, brand kit, placement and intended copy.

423
00:36:08,445 --> 00:36:11,794
Layer zero is deterministic and costs milliseconds.

424
00:36:12,074 --> 00:36:18,112
It checks format, safe zones, and text contrast against wuh-cag, the web accessibility guidelines.

425
00:36:18,392 --> 00:36:26,008
An OCR diff, using optical character recognition, confirms the rendered text matches the approved copy exactly.

426
00:36:26,288 --> 00:36:30,426
Then product fidelity against the catalog image, and logo integrity.

427
00:36:30,706 --> 00:36:33,842
Any hard fail goes straight to reject and regenerate.

428
00:36:34,122 --> 00:36:37,749
Layer one is specialist detectors, tens of milliseconds each.

429
00:36:38,029 --> 00:36:45,538
They cover artifacts, policy, trademarks, and claim NLI, natural language inference against catalog attributes.

430
00:36:45,818 --> 00:36:50,000
Only survivors reach layer two, the VLM judge, which takes seconds.

431
00:36:50,280 --> 00:36:55,506
It scores what rules can't, like brand fit, product prominence and copy-image coherence.

432
00:36:55,786 --> 00:37:00,608
It runs pairwise comparisons with position swap, and writes a structured rationale.

433
00:37:00,888 --> 00:37:05,432
This is the funnel logic of ad serving from chapter four, applied to quality.

434
00:37:06,861 --> 00:37:08,930
Now the bottom half of the same picture.

435
00:37:09,210 --> 00:37:13,882
Judge scores flow into the calibration layer, the key Distinguished-level idea.

436
00:37:14,162 --> 00:37:16,146
Raw scores are not probabilities.

437
00:37:16,426 --> 00:37:24,234
So you fit a mapping, isotonic or Platt, from judge outputs to the probability a human approves, per category and placement.

438
00:37:24,514 --> 00:37:29,144
Now auto-approve above point nine seven has a measurable false-approval rate.

439
00:37:29,424 --> 00:37:32,730
Next comes the router, with cost-asymmetric thresholds.

440
00:37:33,010 --> 00:37:35,912
High probability and no flags, auto-approve.

441
00:37:36,192 --> 00:37:41,482
The middle band goes to human review, plus active-learning picks that teach the judge the most.

442
00:37:41,762 --> 00:37:45,730
Low probability means reject and regenerate with the critique.

443
00:37:46,010 --> 00:37:47,696
Now follow the dotted lines.

444
00:37:47,976 --> 00:37:52,072
A random audit sample of auto-approved items goes back to humans.

445
00:37:52,352 --> 00:37:56,853
Without it, you cannot estimate your false-approval rate, so it's non-negotiable.

446
00:37:57,133 --> 00:38:04,493
Every human decision lands in the label store, which drives weekly recalibration, judge fine-tuning and golden sets.

447
00:38:04,773 --> 00:38:09,594
Finally, drift monitors watch approval rates, score shifts and kappa by segment.

448
00:38:09,874 --> 00:38:13,821
On drift, thresholds tighten automatically, sending more to humans.

449
00:38:15,251 --> 00:38:16,979
On the left, rubric design.

450
00:38:17,259 --> 00:38:19,542
Ask one concrete question per dimension.

451
00:38:19,822 --> 00:38:25,838
Not, is this a good ad, but, is the packaging text identical to the reference, quoting any differences.

452
00:38:26,118 --> 00:38:29,190
Binary checks calibrate better than one-to-five scales.

453
00:38:29,470 --> 00:38:33,950
Always pass references: the catalog image, attributes and brand kit.

454
00:38:34,230 --> 00:38:38,070
Ask for evidence first, then the verdict, as constrained JSON.

455
00:38:38,350 --> 00:38:43,534
Score with the log-probability of the yes token, which is continuous and calibratable.

456
00:38:43,814 --> 00:38:45,968
On the right, biases and defenses.

457
00:38:46,248 --> 00:38:48,595
Position bias: judge both orders.

458
00:38:48,875 --> 00:38:52,715
Self-preference: use a different model family than the generator.

459
00:38:52,995 --> 00:38:55,960
Aesthetic halo: leave fidelity to specialists.

460
00:38:56,240 --> 00:39:00,720
Low-resolution blindness: crop text and product at native resolution.

461
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:05,203
Prompt injection through image text: treat OCR output as data.

462
00:39:06,633 --> 00:39:09,854
The most important single detector is product fidelity.

463
00:39:10,134 --> 00:39:14,956
Align the generated product to the catalog image, then compare inside the mask.

464
00:39:15,236 --> 00:39:25,070
Use DINO or CLIP patch similarity, LPIPS perceptual distance, structural similarity, color delta E, and an OCR diff of the packaging text.

465
00:39:25,350 --> 00:39:28,529
Better still, architect so the judge's job is easy.

466
00:39:28,809 --> 00:39:34,462
Composite the real product with its pixels locked, and fidelity reduces to checking the boundary.

467
00:39:34,742 --> 00:39:41,121
The artifact detector outputs a patch-level heatmap, trained on synthetic corruptions with known masks.

468
00:39:41,401 --> 00:39:43,812
That is your synthetic-data rig experience.

469
00:39:44,092 --> 00:39:47,740
Shadow-direction checks use your spherical-harmonics lighting work.

470
00:39:48,020 --> 00:39:53,118
Claims go through NLI against the catalog, and prices are rendered from live data.

471
00:39:53,398 --> 00:39:57,494
Policy uses classifiers plus a rules engine, tuned for recall.

472
00:39:58,925 --> 00:40:00,930
Calibration is the core keyword.

473
00:40:01,210 --> 00:40:03,365
First, decide what you calibrate to.

474
00:40:03,645 --> 00:40:09,938
Reviewers are the gatekeeping target, advertisers the preference target, and shoppers the performance target.

475
00:40:10,218 --> 00:40:12,394
They disagree, so keep separate heads.

476
00:40:12,674 --> 00:40:13,805
Second, labels.

477
00:40:14,085 --> 00:40:19,162
Use rater guidelines, gold questions, and stratified sampling with recorded weights.

478
00:40:19,442 --> 00:40:22,855
Measure agreement with Cohen's kappa, which corrects for chance.

479
00:40:23,135 --> 00:40:28,981
A judge can't be expected to beat human-to-human agreement, so report both kappas side by side.

480
00:40:29,261 --> 00:40:30,413
Third, the method.

481
00:40:30,693 --> 00:40:35,450
Platt scaling is a logistic regression on the raw score, stable with little data.

482
00:40:35,730 --> 00:40:41,383
Isotonic regression is monotone and flexible, but needs thousands of labels per segment.

483
00:40:41,663 --> 00:40:44,095
Shrink small segments toward the global curve.

484
00:40:44,375 --> 00:40:49,431
Then check E-C-E, the expected calibration error, and precision at the threshold.

485
00:40:50,861 --> 00:40:57,730
For each segment, choose tau, the auto-approve threshold, so the precision of auto-approved items meets a target.

486
00:40:58,010 --> 00:41:03,173
Perhaps ninety-nine percent for critical categories, and ninety-seven for low-risk ones.

487
00:41:03,453 --> 00:41:11,602
Use a lower confidence bound, like a one-sided Wilson bound on held-out labels, because in-sample thresholds are optimistic.

488
00:41:11,882 --> 00:41:14,335
The third line is the expected-cost rule.

489
00:41:14,615 --> 00:41:21,015
Approve only if the probability of bad, times the cost of a bad approval, is below the cost of a review.

490
00:41:21,295 --> 00:41:24,965
Since C bad dwarfs C review, the threshold is conservative.

491
00:41:25,245 --> 00:41:28,338
Those costs come from policy, legal and the business.

492
00:41:29,777 --> 00:41:34,876
For subjective dimensions like appeal, pairwise comparisons beat absolute ratings.

493
00:41:35,156 --> 00:41:41,151
The Bradley-Terry model says the probability that A beats B equals sigma of s A minus s B.

494
00:41:41,431 --> 00:41:48,172
Sigma is the logistic function, and s is a latent preference score for each creative, variant or style.

495
00:41:48,452 --> 00:41:51,204
Elo is the streaming version of the same idea.

496
00:41:51,484 --> 00:41:58,375
Those scores become training data for reward models and for Diffusion DPO, direct preference optimization.

497
00:41:58,655 --> 00:42:00,404
It's drill one in your coding file.

498
00:42:00,684 --> 00:42:04,695
Always show each pair in both orders, so position bias cancels.

499
00:42:06,124 --> 00:42:08,620
Drift detection needs to sound concrete.

500
00:42:08,900 --> 00:42:13,295
Disentangle judge drift from generator drift with two controlled tests.

501
00:42:13,575 --> 00:42:16,967
Re-score a frozen set of creatives with the current judge.

502
00:42:17,247 --> 00:42:20,042
If identical inputs move, the judge drifted.

503
00:42:20,322 --> 00:42:23,970
Then score fresh outputs from frozen prompts with a frozen judge.

504
00:42:24,250 --> 00:42:26,426
If those move, the generator drifted.

505
00:42:26,706 --> 00:42:31,100
On live traffic, compute P-S-I, the population stability index.

506
00:42:31,380 --> 00:42:32,938
Below point one is stable.

507
00:42:33,218 --> 00:42:35,308
Above about point two, investigate.

508
00:42:35,588 --> 00:42:42,586
Run CUSUM, a cumulative-sum change detector, on the audit overturn rate, and canary every change in shadow.

509
00:42:42,866 --> 00:42:44,146
Now reliability.

510
00:42:44,426 --> 00:42:49,055
Fail closed: if the judge is down, route to humans, never to auto-approve.

511
00:42:49,335 --> 00:42:50,743
Let judges abstain.

512
00:42:51,023 --> 00:42:52,644
Pin and version everything.

513
00:42:52,924 --> 00:42:55,676
Keep an audit trail for disputes and regulators.

514
00:42:57,124 --> 00:43:01,754
Goodhart's law says that when a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure.

515
00:43:02,034 --> 00:43:06,215
Use a judge as a reward, and the generator finds its blind spots.

516
00:43:06,495 --> 00:43:07,498
You lived this.

517
00:43:07,778 --> 00:43:15,010
Your reference-free temporal-quality metric for generated video correctly flagged frozen, reversed and shuffled clips.

518
00:43:15,290 --> 00:43:22,991
Used as a training loss, the model learned to satisfy the metric without real improvement, and you published that negative result.

519
00:43:23,271 --> 00:43:24,679
So separate three roles.

520
00:43:24,959 --> 00:43:26,687
The coach is the reward model.

521
00:43:26,967 --> 00:43:29,250
The gatekeeper is the production judges.

522
00:43:29,530 --> 00:43:33,860
The scoreboard is a held-out, human-validated set that nothing trains on.

523
00:43:34,140 --> 00:43:38,002
Add a KL, or Kullback-Leibler, penalty toward the reference model.

524
00:43:38,282 --> 00:43:41,524
Optimize ensemble mean minus lambda times spread.

525
00:43:41,804 --> 00:43:45,239
Divergence between judge and human scores is your alarm.

526
00:43:46,668 --> 00:43:49,740
Close any deep dive by evaluating the evaluator.

527
00:43:50,020 --> 00:43:52,922
These are example targets, not Walmart numbers.

528
00:43:53,202 --> 00:43:57,447
Recall of at least ninety-nine percent on critical failures, for safety.

529
00:43:57,727 --> 00:44:04,682
Auto-approve precision of ninety-seven to ninety-nine percent by risk tier, as a lower confidence bound, for trust.

530
00:44:04,962 --> 00:44:07,906
Coverage maximized subject to both, for cost.

531
00:44:08,186 --> 00:44:14,948
Judge-human kappa within about point zero five of human-human kappa, which means the judge is human-level.

532
00:44:15,228 --> 00:44:20,668
E-C-E below about point zero three, and week-over-week score P-S-I below point one.

533
00:44:20,948 --> 00:44:24,191
The audit overturn rate tracked daily with CUSUM alerts.

534
00:44:24,471 --> 00:44:32,407
Then the headline: review cost per shipped asset down by half or more, while the audited critical-error rate stays below an agreed target.

535
00:44:34,186 --> 00:44:36,810
Chapter seven turns pixels into signals.

536
00:44:37,090 --> 00:44:49,016
The job description asks for structured representations of creative content, attributes, categories and quality signals, that other teams consume for targeting, recommendations and measurement.

537
00:44:49,296 --> 00:44:50,576
This is platform work.

538
00:44:50,856 --> 00:44:56,146
You have spent a career turning pixels into reliable signals that other systems depend on.

539
00:44:57,795 --> 00:45:05,091
In a typical ads stack, a creative is an opaque blob: an ID, an asset location, and a few advertiser fields.

540
00:45:05,371 --> 00:45:11,429
The ranker learns that one creative gets clicks only after spending impressions, and nobody can say why.

541
00:45:11,709 --> 00:45:13,565
A brand-new creative gets nothing.

542
00:45:13,845 --> 00:45:15,488
That's the cold-start problem.

543
00:45:15,768 --> 00:45:21,699
The fix is a creative genome, a versioned record of interpretable attributes for every asset.

544
00:45:21,979 --> 00:45:25,819
Low-level visuals like palette, clutter and text-area ratio.

545
00:45:26,099 --> 00:45:27,955
Semantics like scene and season.

546
00:45:28,235 --> 00:45:30,944
Messaging like offer, price shown and claims.

547
00:45:31,224 --> 00:45:36,323
Then brand, format fit, quality, policy and accessibility, plus dense embeddings.

548
00:45:36,603 --> 00:45:45,435
Use a closed core of fixed values that models can rely on, an open periphery for discovery, and a hierarchy aligned to the product taxonomy.

549
00:45:46,878 --> 00:45:48,734
Extraction is tiered by cost.

550
00:45:49,014 --> 00:45:58,038
Tiers zero and one are deterministic measurements and specialist models: OCR, object and logo detectors, and image-quality models.

551
00:45:58,318 --> 00:46:02,030
Tier two is a CLIP or sig-lip embedding with zero-shot heads.

552
00:46:02,310 --> 00:46:05,489
Use prompt ensembles and include a none-of-these option.

553
00:46:05,769 --> 00:46:13,961
Calibrate each attribute with Platt or isotonic on a few hundred in-domain labels, because raw cosine scores aren't probabilities.

554
00:46:14,241 --> 00:46:19,617
Tier three is a VLM that fills a fixed JSON schema through constrained decoding.

555
00:46:19,897 --> 00:46:23,011
Always allow unknown, or the format forces a guess.

556
00:46:23,291 --> 00:46:24,315
Then distill.

557
00:46:24,595 --> 00:46:33,171
The VLM labels a stratified sample, and a multi-head student on a shared backbone serves every asset, so new attributes are cheap.

558
00:46:33,451 --> 00:46:40,342
And for artifacts, remember chapter six: reference-based checks against the catalog image beat blind detection.

559
00:46:41,772 --> 00:46:43,969
You need both, and here's the line to say.

560
00:46:44,249 --> 00:46:46,511
Embeddings are for machines that predict.

561
00:46:46,791 --> 00:46:50,289
Attributes are for people and systems that reason or act.

562
00:46:50,569 --> 00:46:58,292
Embeddings capture everything, including what nobody named, so they carry the most predictive power and enable similarity search.

563
00:46:58,572 --> 00:47:02,497
But they're opaque, and a new embedding version breaks every consumer.

564
00:47:02,777 --> 00:47:06,596
Attributes are lossy, yet readable, joinable and controllable.

565
00:47:06,876 --> 00:47:14,684
They drive advertiser insights, policy and generation prompts, and their meaning survives model upgrades when the specs are stable.

566
00:47:14,964 --> 00:47:20,532
Ranking uses both, and content features are the big cold-start win for brand-new creatives.

567
00:47:20,812 --> 00:47:29,345
Fine-tune the creative embedding contrastively on creative-to-product, creative-to-query and engagement pairs, with a popularity correction.

568
00:47:30,775 --> 00:47:33,655
Here is the signal platform, Diagram sixteen.

569
00:47:33,935 --> 00:47:35,151
Read it left to right.

570
00:47:35,431 --> 00:47:41,746
First come assets of every origin: catalog, uploaded and generated images, video, and copy.

571
00:47:42,026 --> 00:47:44,991
Next come the extractors, each one a versioned model.

572
00:47:45,271 --> 00:47:55,255
There's OCR and layout, detection, CLIP or sig-lip embeddings, VLM tagging to a fixed schema, quality scores, and video signals like shots and speech.

573
00:47:55,535 --> 00:47:57,711
Above them sits the schema registry.

574
00:47:57,991 --> 00:48:02,535
Every attribute has a definition, allowed values, a version and an owner.

575
00:48:02,815 --> 00:48:06,122
Changing a tagger prompt is a version bump, with a backfill.

576
00:48:06,402 --> 00:48:16,300
Outputs land in a signal store with three shapes: offline tables with point-in-time correctness, an online key-value store for serving, and a vector index.

577
00:48:16,580 --> 00:48:25,476
Quality SLAs, service-level agreements on coverage, freshness and accuracy, make each signal a contract, documented in a signal card.

578
00:48:25,756 --> 00:48:32,327
On the right are six consumers: targeting, ranking, recommendations, measurement, generation and policy.

579
00:48:32,607 --> 00:48:35,039
One extraction investment feeds all six.

580
00:48:35,319 --> 00:48:36,706
That's how it compounds.

581
00:48:38,150 --> 00:48:39,387
Expect this trap.

582
00:48:39,667 --> 00:48:43,891
A dashboard says lifestyle images get fifty percent higher CTR.

583
00:48:44,171 --> 00:48:46,027
That's almost certainly confounded.

584
00:48:46,307 --> 00:48:50,873
Big brands buy agency lifestyle creative and also have brand equity.

585
00:48:51,153 --> 00:48:53,243
Categories differ in base CTR.

586
00:48:53,523 --> 00:48:58,857
Higher bids win better positions, and the ranker already serves creatives it predicts will win.

587
00:48:59,137 --> 00:49:01,995
So use better designs, in order of credibility.

588
00:49:02,275 --> 00:49:10,233
Best is a randomized test of generated variants that differ in exactly one attribute, which generative AI makes cheap.

589
00:49:10,513 --> 00:49:13,585
Next, within-ad-group comparisons with fixed effects.

590
00:49:13,865 --> 00:49:21,267
Then inverse propensity weighting on exploration logs, and double machine learning with cross-fitting, always reporting overlap.

591
00:49:21,547 --> 00:49:27,990
In the interview, say: raw attribute correlations are mostly a map of who can afford agency creative.

592
00:49:29,420 --> 00:49:32,023
Hold the platform accountable at three levels.

593
00:49:32,303 --> 00:49:41,028
Intrinsic quality first: precision, recall and calibration per attribute, on a frozen human gold set plus monthly production audits.

594
00:49:41,308 --> 00:49:47,260
Inter-annotator kappa is your ceiling, and low agreement means the definition is bad, not the model.

595
00:49:47,540 --> 00:49:54,495
Operational next: coverage weighted by spend, freshness against the SLA, and cost per thousand creatives.

596
00:49:54,775 --> 00:49:59,447
Then adoption, meaning how many teams and production models consume the signals.

597
00:49:59,727 --> 00:50:02,351
Finally, downstream lift, the one that matters.

598
00:50:02,631 --> 00:50:09,692
Measure the normalized entropy gain in the click model from adding creative features, sliced to creatives under a day old.

599
00:50:09,972 --> 00:50:14,047
Then confirm online with CTR and ROAS, return on ad spend.

600
00:50:14,327 --> 00:50:17,377
If the signals don't move those, they aren't worth the cost.

601
00:50:19,157 --> 00:50:24,234
Chapter eight covers video ads, from generation to adaptation to evaluation.

602
00:50:24,514 --> 00:50:27,906
The job description names video as a first-class output.

603
00:50:28,186 --> 00:50:33,968
It is also where your background is unusually strong, from SpaceWarp to your temporal-quality metric.

604
00:50:34,248 --> 00:50:38,856
The gap is production video systems and the ad domain, and this chapter closes it.

605
00:50:40,485 --> 00:50:44,218
Start with formats, because every placement is really a constraint set.

606
00:50:44,498 --> 00:50:49,746
Sponsored Videos in search typically autoplay muted, so the visual track carries the story.

607
00:50:50,026 --> 00:50:53,397
That means a hook in the first two seconds and burned-in captions.

608
00:50:53,677 --> 00:50:58,776
Vertical placements need nine by sixteen framing that avoids platform overlays.

609
00:50:59,056 --> 00:51:05,776
Connected TV, or CTV, is the opposite, because it is sound-on, lean-back, and treated like broadcast.

610
00:51:06,056 --> 00:51:11,581
That means fifteen or thirty second spots, title-safe text, and a high-quality master file.

611
00:51:11,861 --> 00:51:19,477
US broadcast loudness targets about minus twenty-four LKFS, a perceptual loudness unit, under the CALM Act.

612
00:51:19,757 --> 00:51:23,682
A social mix near minus fourteen would be rejected at ingest.

613
00:51:23,962 --> 00:51:26,800
So the generator takes the constraint set as input.

614
00:51:28,235 --> 00:51:33,483
Don't start with text-to-video, which has no product grounding and the highest cost and risk.

615
00:51:33,763 --> 00:51:36,750
Instead, tier the system from safest to riskiest.

616
00:51:37,030 --> 00:51:44,112
Tier one is template motion graphics from catalog images, with parallax, kinetic type and a correct end card.

617
00:51:44,392 --> 00:51:50,323
That parallax is depth reprojection plus disocclusion inpainting, exactly your SpaceWarp problem.

618
00:51:50,603 --> 00:51:58,304
Tier two adapts footage the advertiser already has, cutting thirty seconds to fifteen and six with almost no policy risk.

619
00:51:58,584 --> 00:52:05,731
Tier three uses image-to-video for backgrounds and camera motion, with the product composited as a locked, tracked layer.

620
00:52:06,011 --> 00:52:12,198
On cost, assume about twenty GPU-minutes for an undistilled five-second shot, roughly eighty cents.

621
00:52:12,478 --> 00:52:15,038
Distilled to a few steps, it's about four cents.

622
00:52:15,318 --> 00:52:19,414
Templates cost under half a cent, so template-first is the business case.

623
00:52:20,860 --> 00:52:24,977
Here is Diagram seventeen, the video pipeline, read from the top.

624
00:52:25,257 --> 00:52:34,793
The inputs are the SKU, or stock keeping unit, with catalog images, optional brand footage, the brand kit with legal supers, and target durations.

625
00:52:35,073 --> 00:52:41,559
A script language model writes hook, benefit, proof and call to action, grounded on catalog attributes.

626
00:52:41,839 --> 00:52:46,511
The storyboard turns that script into a shot list with timing and on-screen text.

627
00:52:46,791 --> 00:52:51,996
It is the editable intermediate, so advertisers edit the storyboard, not pixels.

628
00:52:52,276 --> 00:53:00,148
Each shot is filled from real footage retrieved by embedding, image-to-video from the locked product image, or a motion template.

629
00:53:00,428 --> 00:53:04,631
Audio uses licensed music and voices, normalized for loudness.

630
00:53:04,911 --> 00:53:08,644
Captions come from the script and are verified by speech recognition.

631
00:53:08,924 --> 00:53:15,601
Edit assembly builds the timeline, which fans out into CTV, vertical, square and six-second versions.

632
00:53:15,881 --> 00:53:20,063
After that come video evaluation, review, and the transcode ladder.

633
00:53:20,343 --> 00:53:25,441
Dotted arrows send failed shots back to the storyboard, and performance back to the script.

634
00:53:26,871 --> 00:53:31,586
In a Sponsored Video, the product is the ad, and video models drift it over time.

635
00:53:31,866 --> 00:53:35,962
A cereal box can morph, or its label can misspell by frame forty.

636
00:53:36,242 --> 00:53:40,807
So track the product through every frame and score identity with DINO features.

637
00:53:41,087 --> 00:53:47,060
Also check packaging text with optical character recognition, plus logo, color and silhouette.

638
00:53:47,340 --> 00:53:52,546
Aggregate with the minimum over frames, not the mean, because one bad frame is visible.

639
00:53:52,826 --> 00:53:57,220
Add a drift term from the first frame, and verify with an independent encoder.

640
00:53:57,500 --> 00:54:05,223
Reframing sixteen by nine to nine by sixteen reuses that tracking, prioritizing product, then text, then faces.

641
00:54:05,503 --> 00:54:10,836
Smooth the crop path with a Kalman or one-euro filter, and reset it at every shot cut.

642
00:54:12,266 --> 00:54:16,405
Video evaluation is a cascade that runs the cheapest checks first.

643
00:54:16,685 --> 00:54:23,085
Spec checks are exact: duration, frame rate, loudness, logo time and legal super duration.

644
00:54:23,365 --> 00:54:31,685
Per-frame checks cover artifacts, aesthetics and moderation, with OCR on every frame, since one bad frame is a policy failure.

645
00:54:31,965 --> 00:54:36,765
Then come temporal checks like warping error against the flow-warped neighbor, and flicker.

646
00:54:37,045 --> 00:54:42,847
Here is the trap: a static video scores perfectly on both, and distilled models often freeze.

647
00:54:43,127 --> 00:54:46,626
So a smoothness score can rise while the clip quietly dies.

648
00:54:46,906 --> 00:54:53,562
Finally, Fréchet Video Distance, or FVD, compares feature statistics across two sets of videos.

649
00:54:53,842 --> 00:54:58,813
It is fine for comparing models, but cannot score the single clip a gate must judge.

650
00:55:00,242 --> 00:55:03,506
This is your published metric, and it fits the gate exactly.

651
00:55:03,786 --> 00:55:11,146
A small frozen network, about five million parameters, looks at two frames and predicts how far apart they are in time.

652
00:55:11,426 --> 00:55:15,799
Delta is the true gap, j minus i, between the two frame indices.

653
00:55:16,079 --> 00:55:20,495
The score is the average log-likelihood of those true gaps under the predictor.

654
00:55:20,775 --> 00:55:24,125
Real motion makes gaps predictable, so the score is high.

655
00:55:24,405 --> 00:55:30,378
Near-static clips look like zero gap everywhere, while reversed and shuffled clips break the arrow of time.

656
00:55:30,658 --> 00:55:34,050
All three score low, with no reference video set needed.

657
00:55:34,330 --> 00:55:37,615
But used as a training loss, the generator learned to game it.

658
00:55:39,045 --> 00:55:43,568
When I used my temporal metric as a training loss, the generator learned to game it.

659
00:55:43,848 --> 00:55:47,496
Any evaluator you optimize against stops being an evaluator.

660
00:55:47,776 --> 00:55:56,266
So I keep a held-out evaluator the generator never sees, ensemble diverse metrics, and alarm when they diverge from human ratings.

661
00:55:56,546 --> 00:56:01,474
Use this whenever reward models or best-of-N selection come up in the interview.

662
00:56:03,270 --> 00:56:04,742
Welcome to chapter nine.

663
00:56:05,022 --> 00:56:09,395
You have seen how to generate creative, judge it, and describe it as signals.

664
00:56:09,675 --> 00:56:13,707
Now we connect those pieces into a system that learns from shoppers.

665
00:56:13,987 --> 00:56:18,531
Three diagrams: the optimization loop, the shared platform, and the roadmap.

666
00:56:18,811 --> 00:56:22,267
This is your vision answer, for when they ask what you would build here.

667
00:56:23,897 --> 00:56:27,566
Here is Diagram eighteen, the creative optimization loop.

668
00:56:27,846 --> 00:56:29,062
Read it left to right.

669
00:56:29,342 --> 00:56:35,337
First, generate four to eight variants per ad, deliberately diverse in scene type and copy angle.

670
00:56:35,617 --> 00:56:38,646
If every variant looks the same, the loop learns nothing.

671
00:56:38,926 --> 00:56:43,726
Tag each variant with its attributes, so learning transfers across advertisers.

672
00:56:44,006 --> 00:56:46,950
Next come the evaluation gates from the judge chapter.

673
00:56:47,230 --> 00:56:49,449
Only safe, on-brand variants pass.

674
00:56:49,729 --> 00:56:53,356
Here's the key idea: exploration never relaxes safety.

675
00:56:53,636 --> 00:56:54,553
Then serving.

676
00:56:54,833 --> 00:57:00,785
Surviving variants compete inside their ad group using Thompson sampling, per ad and per placement.

677
00:57:01,065 --> 00:57:10,580
Most ads get little traffic, so hierarchical priors, pooled across advertisers by creative attribute, give a new variant a sensible starting belief.

678
00:57:10,860 --> 00:57:12,545
That is your cold start answer.

679
00:57:12,825 --> 00:57:17,006
Outcomes come back as impressions, clicks and conversions by variant.

680
00:57:17,286 --> 00:57:19,121
Before learning anything, debias.

681
00:57:19,401 --> 00:57:23,945
Raw click differences are confounded by position, placement and audience mix.

682
00:57:24,225 --> 00:57:28,449
Skip this, and the loop simply learns that variant B got the top slot more often.

683
00:57:29,897 --> 00:57:32,244
Thompson sampling is easy to explain.

684
00:57:32,524 --> 00:57:39,009
Treat each variant's click-through rate, or CTR, as unknown, with a Beta distribution as your belief.

685
00:57:39,289 --> 00:57:44,580
Alpha k is a prior pseudo-count, alpha zero, plus the clicks variant k has earned.

686
00:57:44,860 --> 00:57:48,422
Beta k is beta zero plus impressions without a click.

687
00:57:48,702 --> 00:57:54,804
At each impression, draw one sample, theta k, from every variant's Beta, and show the largest draw.

688
00:57:55,084 --> 00:57:58,540
Uncertain variants sometimes draw high, so they get explored.

689
00:57:58,820 --> 00:58:02,041
Proven winners usually draw high, so they get exploited.

690
00:58:02,321 --> 00:58:05,905
Exploration is automatically proportional to uncertainty.

691
00:58:07,355 --> 00:58:09,062
Same diagram, right half.

692
00:58:09,342 --> 00:58:16,233
Debiased data trains a creative performance model that predicts lift from creative features plus context.

693
00:58:16,513 --> 00:58:22,571
A reward model combines predicted performance, judge quality and policy constraints into one score.

694
00:58:22,851 --> 00:58:28,099
Winner and loser variants for the same product and context form clean preference pairs.

695
00:58:28,379 --> 00:58:30,129
How does the generator improve?

696
00:58:30,409 --> 00:58:36,062
Cheapest first: update the prompt and conditioning policy, meaning which scenes the planner requests.

697
00:58:36,342 --> 00:58:40,950
Next, best-of-N: generate several candidates and keep the top-scoring one.

698
00:58:41,230 --> 00:58:43,449
Small N means bounded exploitation.

699
00:58:43,729 --> 00:58:50,193
Last, Diffusion-DPO, direct preference optimization adapted to diffusion, trained on those pairs.

700
00:58:50,473 --> 00:58:51,539
Now the guards.

701
00:58:51,819 --> 00:58:59,478
A KL penalty, the Kullback-Leibler divergence to a reference generator, stops collapse onto weird high-reward outputs.

702
00:58:59,758 --> 00:59:01,507
Hard gates are never bypassed.

703
00:59:01,787 --> 00:59:09,531
And keep three roles separate: the reward you optimize, the gatekeeper judges, and a held-out human scoreboard that nothing trains on.

704
00:59:09,811 --> 00:59:14,761
Your negative result, where a generator gamed your video metric, is exactly why.

705
00:59:15,041 --> 00:59:20,929
Finally, reward incremental conversion, because click-optimized creative drifts toward clickbait.

706
00:59:22,358 --> 00:59:25,900
Diagram nineteen shows the shared platform in three layers.

707
00:59:26,180 --> 00:59:39,769
On top sit product applications: the Automated Creative Generator, the Marty advertiser agent, the Sponsored Brands builder, DSP and connected TV tools, catalog image enhancement, and Sparky formats.

708
00:59:40,049 --> 00:59:44,614
In the middle are shared creative services, the layer this role should own or shape.

709
00:59:44,894 --> 00:59:49,929
A generation gateway handles model routing, quotas, caching and prompt templates.

710
00:59:50,209 --> 00:59:55,926
An evaluation harness runs offline benchmarks and the online judge with the same rubric.

711
00:59:56,206 --> 01:00:03,929
Then the signal store, policy service, asset store with lineage, the bandit service, and review tooling with a shared label store.

712
01:00:04,209 --> 01:00:10,033
At the bottom: GPU pool, model registry, feature store, data lake and observability.

713
01:00:10,313 --> 01:00:11,700
Why does this compound?

714
01:00:11,980 --> 01:00:19,190
Every new product launches with evaluation, policy and measurement for free, and every label improves every product.

715
01:00:19,470 --> 01:00:21,924
Swapping a model becomes a config change.

716
01:00:22,204 --> 01:00:28,625
Tie it to Golf Mode: one fused model beat per-sensor pipelines, and one platform beats six silos.

717
01:00:30,067 --> 01:00:32,733
Diagram twenty is the sequenced roadmap.

718
01:00:33,013 --> 01:00:36,683
Open with a hedge: you would spend the first weeks learning what exists.

719
01:00:36,963 --> 01:00:39,181
In the first ninety days, measure first.

720
01:00:39,461 --> 01:00:49,744
Audit the pipeline and costs, build golden sets with agreement targets, and calibrate judge version zero on existing review decisions, which is free labeled data.

721
01:00:50,024 --> 01:00:57,939
Your first visible win is adaptation: take approved assets and automatically produce more placements, behind fidelity gates.

722
01:00:58,219 --> 01:01:01,440
Low hallucination risk, high advertiser value.

723
01:01:01,720 --> 01:01:07,779
Quarter two makes the eval harness a shared service and turns on auto-approve for the lowest-risk tier.

724
01:01:08,059 --> 01:01:11,173
Quarter three adds bandit serving and video version one.

725
01:01:11,453 --> 01:01:16,168
Quarter four closes the loop with the performance model and a learned prompt policy.

726
01:01:16,448 --> 01:01:18,880
Year two brings preference-tuned generators.

727
01:01:19,160 --> 01:01:21,144
Notice the arrows are dependencies.

728
01:01:21,424 --> 01:01:32,816
In the interview, say: every later step is only as good as our ability to measure quality, so eval comes first, and each quarter ships a win, from review latency to incremental ROAS.

729
01:01:34,596 --> 01:01:37,028
Chapter ten: system design walkthroughs.

730
01:01:37,308 --> 01:01:40,017
This is where the interview often spends the most time.

731
01:01:40,297 --> 01:01:43,753
You will learn one framework, then walk six creative designs.

732
01:01:44,033 --> 01:01:52,033
We go deepest on the first, generating Sponsored Brands and display creative from a product, because it touches every pillar of the role.

733
01:01:52,313 --> 01:01:54,361
The other five take a minute or two each.

734
01:01:55,996 --> 01:02:01,073
Use the same seven steps every time, and say them out loud so the interviewer can follow.

735
01:02:01,353 --> 01:02:08,137
Clarify, metrics, architecture, deep dive, scale and cost, evaluation and launch, then risks and roadmap.

736
01:02:08,417 --> 01:02:21,494
For a forty-five minute design, budget about five minutes to clarify, five for metrics, eight for architecture, fifteen for the deep dive, five for scale, four for evaluation and launch, and three for the roadmap.

737
01:02:21,774 --> 01:02:24,377
Notice the deep dive gets a third of the time.

738
01:02:24,657 --> 01:02:31,633
For this role, always cover three more things: safety by design, the judge stack, and what becomes shared platform.

739
01:02:31,913 --> 01:02:34,494
And hit the Distinguished signals in every design.

740
01:02:34,774 --> 01:02:41,345
State tradeoffs explicitly, name what you would not build yet, do a back-of-envelope number, and end with the roadmap.

741
01:02:42,787 --> 01:02:52,601
Design one: generate Sponsored Brands and display creative for any advertiser from a product SKU, the stock keeping unit, meaning one catalog item.

742
01:02:52,881 --> 01:02:54,715
Start with three or four questions.

743
01:02:54,995 --> 01:03:00,585
Is this for national brands with brand kits, or long-tail sellers with only catalog images?

744
01:03:00,865 --> 01:03:03,851
The answer is both, and the long tail benefits most.

745
01:03:04,131 --> 01:03:05,155
Which formats?

746
01:03:05,435 --> 01:03:10,555
Banners, I-A-B display sizes, vertical social, and connected TV end cards.

747
01:03:10,835 --> 01:03:12,201
Interactive or bulk?

748
01:03:12,481 --> 01:03:18,497
Interactive needs a preview in roughly five to twenty seconds, while bulk is throughput-optimized overnight.

749
01:03:18,777 --> 01:03:20,313
Then metrics, in groups.

750
01:03:20,593 --> 01:03:21,489
Adoption.

751
01:03:21,769 --> 01:03:26,483
Quality and safety, like critical-failure rate and human-review overturn rate.

752
01:03:26,763 --> 01:03:33,995
Performance, meaning CTR and CVR, the conversion rate, measured in randomized tests within an ad group.

753
01:03:34,275 --> 01:03:39,886
And efficiency: dollars and GPU-seconds per shipped asset, not per generated asset.

754
01:03:40,166 --> 01:03:43,451
Add guardrails, so CTR never comes from clickbait.

755
01:03:44,881 --> 01:03:47,655
Now draw the architecture, Diagram fourteen.

756
01:03:47,935 --> 01:03:53,759
Inputs on top: the SKU, the brand kit, the campaign goal, target placements, and an optional brief.

757
01:03:54,039 --> 01:03:58,945
The catalog fetch pulls hero images, title, attributes and approved claims.

758
01:03:59,225 --> 01:04:02,489
The catalog is the source of truth for pixels and words.

759
01:04:02,769 --> 01:04:09,297
A planner LLM, a large language model with structured output, turns the brief into several concepts.

760
01:04:09,577 --> 01:04:10,815
Then two branches.

761
01:04:11,095 --> 01:04:14,423
Visually, the product is segmented, matted, and locked.

762
01:04:14,703 --> 01:04:21,892
Diffusion generates the scene around it, with depth or edge control for perspective and a style adapter for the brand look.

763
01:04:22,172 --> 01:04:25,159
Harmonization matches lighting, shadows and color.

764
01:04:25,439 --> 01:04:33,524
For copy, an LLM grounded in catalog attributes writes headlines, and a claim checker traces every claim to an attribute.

765
01:04:33,804 --> 01:04:41,441
The composition engine places layers, logo and text deterministically, and per-placement adaptation makes every size.

766
01:04:41,721 --> 01:04:47,353
Then the judge stack, a router, advertiser approval, an asset store with lineage, and serving.

767
01:04:47,633 --> 01:04:50,897
Performance logs by creative ID feed the learning loop.

768
01:04:52,340 --> 01:04:54,430
If they ask you to go deep, start here.

769
01:04:54,710 --> 01:04:58,038
Why generate the background and compose deterministically?

770
01:04:58,318 --> 01:05:02,798
First, the product pixels are real, so fidelity holds by construction.

771
01:05:03,078 --> 01:05:05,958
The judges only need to verify the boundary.

772
01:05:06,238 --> 01:05:09,481
Second, text, logo and price are rendered by code.

773
01:05:09,761 --> 01:05:24,140
That means no garbled text, exact brand fonts, prices pulled live from pricing data, and exact contrast checks. wuh-cag, the web accessibility guidelines, asks for four point five to one contrast for normal text.

774
01:05:24,420 --> 01:05:29,796
Third, the generative model does what it is good at: scenes, mood, lighting and context.

775
01:05:30,076 --> 01:05:31,676
Be honest about the tradeoff.

776
01:05:31,956 --> 01:05:35,881
You lose some magical integration, like a hand holding the product.

777
01:05:36,161 --> 01:05:42,028
Handle that with harmonization now, and later with a stricter-judged tier of full-generative scenes.

778
01:05:43,465 --> 01:05:45,022
More deep dives you can own.

779
01:05:45,302 --> 01:05:46,945
Harmonization is your edge.

780
01:05:47,225 --> 01:05:53,113
Estimate the scene's lighting with spherical harmonics, just like your three-sixty lighting estimation work.

781
01:05:53,393 --> 01:06:02,587
Relight the matted product with a bounded shading ratio so identity is preserved, add a contact shadow from the light direction, and match color temperature.

782
01:06:02,867 --> 01:06:04,958
Without it, composites look pasted.

783
01:06:05,238 --> 01:06:06,859
Next, brand conditioning.

784
01:06:07,139 --> 01:06:14,329
With thousands of brands, a LoRA per brand, meaning a low-rank adapter, carries real training and swapping cost.

785
01:06:14,609 --> 01:06:22,459
Default to reference-based style conditioning plus palette checks, and reserve Loras for top-spend brands where evals show a gap.

786
01:06:22,739 --> 01:06:30,910
For copy, generate from catalog attributes within placement limits, then verify claims with NLI, natural language inference.

787
01:06:31,190 --> 01:06:40,619
For variants, generate several concepts and renders, judge them, keep a diverse top few, let the advertiser pick, and let the bandit decide in serving.

788
01:06:42,049 --> 01:06:45,398
Now the back-of-envelope, and state your assumptions out loud.

789
01:06:45,678 --> 01:06:52,889
One million SKUs, times five placements, times four variants, equals twenty million assets per full refresh.

790
01:06:53,169 --> 01:06:58,950
A few-step distilled model takes about one to two GPU-seconds per one-megapixel image.

791
01:06:59,230 --> 01:07:07,379
That gives twenty to forty million GPU-seconds, or about five thousand five hundred to eleven thousand GPU-hours per refresh.

792
01:07:07,659 --> 01:07:11,606
A standard thirty to fifty step sampler is roughly ten times worse.

793
01:07:11,886 --> 01:07:13,443
The conclusion is the point.

794
01:07:13,723 --> 01:07:15,515
Never regenerate everything.

795
01:07:15,795 --> 01:07:18,142
Generate on demand for active campaigns.

796
01:07:18,422 --> 01:07:22,454
Cache by SKU image, concept, template and model version.

797
01:07:22,734 --> 01:07:27,257
Adapt existing renders across placements with cheap outpainting or cropping.

798
01:07:27,537 --> 01:07:33,553
For interactive use, show a fast low-resolution preview, then render full resolution on selection.

799
01:07:33,833 --> 01:07:36,755
Your consistency model experience pays off right here.

800
01:07:38,185 --> 01:07:41,235
Design two is the automated evaluation system.

801
01:07:41,515 --> 01:07:44,374
You already have it from the judge chapter, chapter six.

802
01:07:44,654 --> 01:07:57,454
Structure it as beats: failure-mode taxonomy, the judge cascade, labels, calibration, routing economics, drift, fail-closed reliability, Goodhart separation, and the harness as a shared service.

803
01:07:57,734 --> 01:08:05,329
Close with a number: review cost per shipped asset down fifty percent or more, while the audited critical-error rate stays below target.

804
01:08:05,609 --> 01:08:08,105
Design three is video, from chapter eight.

805
01:08:08,385 --> 01:08:09,537
Recommend tiers.

806
01:08:09,817 --> 01:08:15,470
Tier one is deterministic motion graphics from stills, like parallax from monocular depth.

807
01:08:15,750 --> 01:08:17,969
It is safe and cheap, so ship it first.

808
01:08:18,249 --> 01:08:26,142
Tier two adapts existing video: cut-downs from thirty to fifteen to six seconds, and subject-tracked reframing to vertical.

809
01:08:26,422 --> 01:08:31,777
Tier three is generative motion, with the product composited as a locked, tracked layer.

810
01:08:32,057 --> 01:08:35,897
Your temporal metric becomes the frozen and reversed clip detector.

811
01:08:37,327 --> 01:08:41,401
Design four is creative understanding signals, from chapter seven.

812
01:08:41,681 --> 01:08:47,143
Clarify the consumers first: ranking, targeting, measurement, policy and generation.

813
01:08:47,423 --> 01:08:53,887
Then a versioned schema, and VLM extraction, meaning a vision language model, distilled into cheap students.

814
01:08:54,167 --> 01:08:59,116
Measure adoption by downstream lift, like better click prediction for new creatives.

815
01:08:59,396 --> 01:09:02,724
And warn that raw attribute correlations are confounded.

816
01:09:03,004 --> 01:09:11,281
Design five is the closed loop from chapter nine: Thompson sampling, a performance model, preference tuning, and reward-hacking guards.

817
01:09:11,561 --> 01:09:15,209
Design six adapts one master creative to thirty placements.

818
01:09:15,489 --> 01:09:18,220
Work from layers, or decompose a flat image.

819
01:09:18,500 --> 01:09:27,737
Pick a layout family, extend with outpainting or saliency crop, re-place elements with a constraint solver, shorten copy, and enforce contrast.

820
01:09:28,017 --> 01:09:35,271
Call it the fastest first win: low hallucination risk, high advertiser value, and it reuses approved assets.

821
01:09:36,702 --> 01:09:38,003
Finally, practice.

822
01:09:38,283 --> 01:09:40,267
Detect and fix distorted text.

823
01:09:40,547 --> 01:09:43,470
Generate accurate, wuh-cag-compliant alt text.

824
01:09:43,750 --> 01:09:46,395
Design the human review and labeling program.

825
01:09:46,675 --> 01:09:49,875
Personalize creative for Sparky conversational ads.

826
01:09:50,155 --> 01:09:56,640
Debug an auto-approve precision drop from ninety-eight point five to ninety-five percent in one week.

827
01:09:56,920 --> 01:10:00,291
Handle a brand complaint about a misrepresented product.

828
01:10:00,571 --> 01:10:03,856
Turn a PDF style guide into enforceable rules.

829
01:10:04,136 --> 01:10:06,974
And decide build versus buy for the image model.

830
01:10:07,254 --> 01:10:08,640
Here is how to rehearse.

831
01:10:08,920 --> 01:10:12,206
Set a forty-minute timer and speak out loud, not in your head.

832
01:10:12,486 --> 01:10:14,363
Name the seven steps as you go.

833
01:10:14,643 --> 01:10:16,222
Draw the boxes on paper.

834
01:10:16,502 --> 01:10:18,208
Glance at the clock at each step.

835
01:10:18,488 --> 01:10:20,110
Always finish with the roadmap.

836
01:10:20,390 --> 01:10:22,459
Do designs one and two at least twice.

837
01:10:24,239 --> 01:10:25,732
This chapter is about you.

838
01:10:26,012 --> 01:10:30,876
Every technical chapter so far feeds into a few stories you will tell out loud.

839
01:10:31,156 --> 01:10:39,583
We will build your ninety-second pitch, your answer to what you would build here, your first ninety days, and which story answers which behavioral question.

840
01:10:41,222 --> 01:10:44,977
In face synthesis the hard constraint is, don't change who this is.

841
01:10:45,257 --> 01:10:48,307
In ad creative it's, don't change what this product is.

842
01:10:48,587 --> 01:10:55,179
And I published a reference-free metric for generated video, then a negative result when a model learned to game it.

843
01:10:55,459 --> 01:10:58,361
That shapes how I'd build a model-as-judge system here.

844
01:10:58,641 --> 01:11:01,713
This is the core of your answer to tell me about yourself.

845
01:11:03,142 --> 01:11:07,387
The full pitch has five beats, and each beat is only one or two sentences.

846
01:11:07,667 --> 01:11:15,582
Open with who you are: a Principal Engineer who has built generative vision systems from scratch and shipped them under hard constraints.

847
01:11:15,862 --> 01:11:22,497
Give one proof point: your motion-vector SpaceWarp beat Google's and became the default on Galaxy XR.

848
01:11:22,777 --> 01:11:24,291
Then two matches to the role.

849
01:11:24,571 --> 01:11:30,161
First, faithful generation, using masks, lighting harmonization and identity losses.

850
01:11:30,441 --> 01:11:35,134
Second, evaluation, where your negative result shows you know judges get gamed.

851
01:11:35,414 --> 01:11:43,030
Close with why Walmart: you can generate, judge, serve and learn from real shopper behavior, across thousands of brands.

852
01:11:43,310 --> 01:11:49,113
Practice until it lands at eighty-five to ninety-five seconds and sounds natural, not memorized.

853
01:11:50,559 --> 01:11:51,945
Start with the north star.

854
01:11:52,225 --> 01:12:01,185
Every advertiser, from a national brand to a small marketplace seller, gets on-brand, compliant, high-performing creative for every placement.

855
01:12:01,465 --> 01:12:03,684
Then five pillars, one sentence each.

856
01:12:03,964 --> 01:12:12,348
One, faithful generation by construction: real product pixels locked, generated backgrounds, deterministic text and layout.

857
01:12:12,628 --> 01:12:21,972
Two, a judge stack, not a judge: cheap checks, then specialist detectors, then a VLM rubric judge, then calibration and risk-based routing.

858
01:12:22,252 --> 01:12:29,591
Three, a creative genome: attributes and embeddings for every creative, served to ranking, targeting and measurement.

859
01:12:29,871 --> 01:12:35,759
Four, closed-loop optimization, keeping the optimization reward separate from the safety judges.

860
01:12:36,039 --> 01:12:39,303
Five, one shared platform every product composes on.

861
01:12:39,583 --> 01:12:41,524
Go deep only on the pillar they pick.

862
01:12:42,954 --> 01:12:44,362
Say the roadmap this way.

863
01:12:44,642 --> 01:12:47,031
Months zero to three: measure first.

864
01:12:47,311 --> 01:12:56,036
Build the eval harness, golden sets and failure catalog, and ship a quick win by adapting existing assets to more placements with fidelity gates.

865
01:12:56,316 --> 01:13:05,874
Months three to nine: judge version one in production, genome version one with one real consumer, and image generation with lighting harmonization.

866
01:13:06,154 --> 01:13:11,124
Months nine to eighteen: video, closed-loop tuning, and advertiser insights.

867
01:13:11,404 --> 01:13:13,580
Then personalization at serving time.

868
01:13:13,860 --> 01:13:15,866
Chapter nine covers this in depth.

869
01:13:16,146 --> 01:13:18,983
Just as important, name what you would not do first.

870
01:13:19,263 --> 01:13:23,615
No fully generative product imagery, because of hallucination risk.

871
01:13:23,895 --> 01:13:26,732
Never train the generator against the gating judge.

872
01:13:27,012 --> 01:13:33,732
And no per-brand LoRA fine-tunes, meaning small adapter models, until reference adapters fall short.

873
01:13:35,162 --> 01:13:38,255
For the first ninety days, show humility and then speed.

874
01:13:38,535 --> 01:13:40,839
In days one to thirty, listen and measure.

875
01:13:41,119 --> 01:13:47,946
Meet product, engineering, ads ranking, policy, brand safety, measurement, and a few real advertisers.

876
01:13:48,226 --> 01:13:51,383
Read the rejection logs to learn what fails today and why.

877
01:13:51,663 --> 01:13:55,247
Collect one to two thousand labeled examples per failure mode.

878
01:13:55,527 --> 01:14:06,557
In days thirty-one to sixty, publish the eval contract: a quality taxonomy, a golden-set process, and a judge version zero with its agreement against humans reported.

879
01:14:06,837 --> 01:14:12,149
In days sixty-one to ninety, deliver an eighteen-month strategy and one measurable win.

880
01:14:12,429 --> 01:14:17,207
For example, cut human review volume by forty percent at equal precision.

881
01:14:17,487 --> 01:14:21,797
That order, measure before you build, is what Distinguished judgment looks like.

882
01:14:23,245 --> 01:14:24,589
Here is your story map.

883
01:14:24,869 --> 01:14:30,821
For biggest impact, tell SpaceWarp, and end on the lesson that head-to-head evaluation decided it.

884
01:14:31,101 --> 01:14:36,051
For evaluation, or for a failure, tell the temporal metric and its negative result.

885
01:14:36,331 --> 01:14:37,824
Stress that you published it.

886
01:14:38,104 --> 01:14:44,653
For taking generation to production, tell Passthrough Eyes, with its fidelity constraints and latency budget.

887
01:14:44,933 --> 01:14:50,629
For influence without authority, tell Golf Mode or your OpenXR extension proposal.

888
01:14:50,909 --> 01:14:54,899
For a tradeoff with executives, use your E-V-P award demos.

889
01:14:55,179 --> 01:14:57,163
Fill in real numbers before Monday.

890
01:14:57,443 --> 01:14:59,107
Then avoid the classic traps.

891
01:14:59,387 --> 01:15:01,691
Keep XR detail to one sentence.

892
01:15:01,971 --> 01:15:03,123
Say I, not we.

893
01:15:03,403 --> 01:15:05,045
Never bluff ads specifics.

894
01:15:05,325 --> 01:15:07,800
Tie every metric to dollars or shoppers.

895
01:15:08,080 --> 01:15:09,595
Never criticize Samsung.

896
01:15:11,037 --> 01:15:24,562
Accessibility is a preferred qualification most candidates will skip, so claim it. wuh-cag two point two, level double A, needs text contrast of at least four point five to one, or three to one for large text.

897
01:15:24,842 --> 01:15:28,405
Images need text alternatives, and videos need captions.

898
01:15:28,685 --> 01:15:31,714
Content should not flash more than three times per second.

899
01:15:31,994 --> 01:15:33,594
Then make it creative AI.

900
01:15:33,874 --> 01:15:38,845
Generate alt text for every ad image with a VLM grounded in the catalog.

901
01:15:39,125 --> 01:15:41,770
Make contrast a hard gate in the layout engine.

902
01:15:42,050 --> 01:15:43,992
Auto-caption every video ad.

903
01:15:44,272 --> 01:15:53,616
In the interview, say: accessibility is a judge dimension in my eval stack, and it also improves performance, because captions help muted autoplay.

904
01:15:55,395 --> 01:15:58,872
Welcome to chapter twelve, where the packet turns into practice.

905
01:15:59,152 --> 01:16:03,376
Your question bank has twenty-five starred questions, the most likely ones.

906
01:16:03,656 --> 01:16:06,942
We will model ten of them, weighted toward model-as-judge.

907
01:16:07,222 --> 01:16:09,056
Listen for the shape of each answer.

908
01:16:09,336 --> 01:16:12,728
Then we cover the coding drills, and the five to rehearse first.

909
01:16:14,378 --> 01:16:21,760
Creative intelligence is the loop of generate, evaluate, understand, deploy, and learn, applied to ad creative.

910
01:16:22,040 --> 01:16:24,216
For thirty days, I listen and audit.

911
01:16:24,496 --> 01:16:30,234
By day sixty, a versioned evaluation harness, a human gold set, and a calibrated judge.

912
01:16:30,514 --> 01:16:34,056
By day ninety, a sequenced roadmap plus one shipped win.

913
01:16:34,336 --> 01:16:36,512
Use this to open any vision question.

914
01:16:37,962 --> 01:16:41,204
No single detector catches everything, so I layer them.

915
01:16:41,484 --> 01:16:46,519
Specialist artifact classifiers trained on hard negatives from our own generator.

916
01:16:46,799 --> 01:16:50,490
A PatchGAN-style patch map showing where the image is wrong.

917
01:16:50,770 --> 01:16:53,970
Structural checks like text recognition and keypoints.

918
01:16:54,250 --> 01:16:57,172
And a calibrated VLM, a vision language model.

919
01:16:57,452 --> 01:16:59,778
Then mention the discriminators you have trained.

920
01:17:01,212 --> 01:17:04,732
This is the center of the job description, so expect it early.

921
01:17:05,012 --> 01:17:09,705
Open with the structure: a cascade with three jobs, gate, score, and monitor.

922
01:17:09,985 --> 01:17:19,628
First, deterministic validators for format, safe zones, contrast, and whether OCR, optical character recognition, matches the approved copy.

923
01:17:19,908 --> 01:17:21,358
They are cheap and exact.

924
01:17:21,638 --> 01:17:25,649
Second, specialist models for fidelity, artifacts, and policy.

925
01:17:25,929 --> 01:17:31,646
Third, a VLM judge with a decomposed rubric, returning structured scores and evidence.

926
01:17:31,926 --> 01:17:40,950
Aggregate calibrated scores into pass, review, or block, using cost-sensitive thresholds, with the middle band sized to reviewer capacity.

927
01:17:41,230 --> 01:17:45,177
Close with a human gold set, frozen canaries, and versioned verdicts.

928
01:17:45,457 --> 01:17:47,228
Every verdict should be replayable.

929
01:17:47,508 --> 01:17:49,470
Say you would build the gold set first.

930
01:17:50,920 --> 01:17:52,541
Four steps, said in order.

931
01:17:52,821 --> 01:18:00,544
First, collect a stratified human-labeled set, sliced by category, format, advertiser size, and generator version.

932
01:18:00,824 --> 01:18:04,877
Measure how often raters agree with each other, because that is your ceiling.

933
01:18:05,157 --> 01:18:13,797
Second, get a continuous score from the judge, such as the probability-weighted expected rating, not the sampled integer, which bunches in the middle.

934
01:18:14,077 --> 01:18:22,056
Third, fit a monotone map from score to the chance a human approves, using isotonic regression or Platt scaling, per slice.

935
01:18:22,336 --> 01:18:25,259
Check E-C-E, the expected calibration error.

936
01:18:25,539 --> 01:18:29,336
Fourth, report Cohen's kappa next to the human-to-human kappa.

937
01:18:29,616 --> 01:18:31,344
A judge at that ceiling is done.

938
01:18:31,624 --> 01:18:36,467
Your angle: ArcFace similarity only meant something once mapped to labeled pairs.

939
01:18:37,896 --> 01:18:39,475
Three drifts, three fixes.

940
01:18:39,755 --> 01:18:44,661
Judge drift: re-score a frozen canary set, so any change must be the judge.

941
01:18:44,941 --> 01:18:50,851
Input drift: P-S-I, the population stability index, or a KS test on embeddings.

942
01:18:51,131 --> 01:18:54,992
Concept drift: fresh human labels on a small random sample.

943
01:18:55,272 --> 01:18:56,723
And pin the judge version.

944
01:18:57,003 --> 01:18:59,733
Admit you have not run a production LLM judge.

945
01:19:01,163 --> 01:19:06,198
This is your strongest question, and it doubles as your story about a metric that was gamed.

946
01:19:06,478 --> 01:19:07,779
Start with the principle.

947
01:19:08,059 --> 01:19:15,739
Optimization pressure amplifies any gap between proxy and target, so assume the generator finds the judge's blind spots.

948
01:19:16,019 --> 01:19:17,214
Then the defenses.

949
01:19:17,494 --> 01:19:21,206
Keep the training judge separate from the final evaluation judge.

950
01:19:21,486 --> 01:19:23,022
Use diverse ensembles.

951
01:19:23,302 --> 01:19:31,344
Constrain optimization with a KL penalty, the Kullback Leibler divergence to the base model, or best of N with a modest N.

952
01:19:31,624 --> 01:19:33,950
Have humans audit the top-scoring outputs.

953
01:19:34,230 --> 01:19:35,488
Then tell your story.

954
01:19:35,768 --> 01:19:44,003
Your temporal-quality metric, used as a training loss, was satisfied without real improvement, and you published that negative result.

955
01:19:44,283 --> 01:19:48,080
That is why a deployment judge never doubles as a training reward.

956
01:19:49,510 --> 01:19:52,433
One creative schema per asset, with four layers.

957
01:19:52,713 --> 01:19:54,185
Identity and lineage.

958
01:19:54,465 --> 01:19:57,494
Versioned content attributes, each with a confidence.

959
01:19:57,774 --> 01:20:00,249
Calibrated quality and compliance signals.

960
01:20:00,529 --> 01:20:01,510
And embeddings.

961
01:20:01,790 --> 01:20:08,809
Ranking uses the signals as features, targeting uses the attributes, and measurement uses them as lift dimensions.

962
01:20:09,089 --> 01:20:12,673
Then add your fusion story about one shared representation.

963
01:20:14,102 --> 01:20:15,553
Layers at every stage.

964
01:20:15,833 --> 01:20:18,030
Screen inputs before spending compute.

965
01:20:18,310 --> 01:20:22,321
Constrain generation, and never free-generate regulated claims.

966
01:20:22,601 --> 01:20:26,291
Run policy classifiers with recall-guaranteed thresholds.

967
01:20:26,571 --> 01:20:28,918
Keep human review for borderline cases.

968
01:20:29,198 --> 01:20:33,721
After serving, monitor, with a kill switch for any asset or generator version.

969
01:20:34,001 --> 01:20:37,905
Policy lives as versioned rules, so changes become config.

970
01:20:39,352 --> 01:20:43,320
This is the ads performance question, where you show you have learned the business.

971
01:20:43,600 --> 01:20:48,016
The answer is a randomized experiment designed with the auction in mind.

972
01:20:48,296 --> 01:20:56,701
Randomize impressions or users between the advertiser's creative and the generated one, holding bids, targeting, and budget fixed.

973
01:20:56,981 --> 01:21:03,659
Measure CTR, the click-through rate, plus conversion, attributed sales, and ROAS, the return on ad spend.

974
01:21:03,939 --> 01:21:05,731
Now the insight that earns credit.

975
01:21:06,011 --> 01:21:13,136
A better creative raises eCPM, the effective revenue per thousand impressions, so it wins more auctions.

976
01:21:13,416 --> 01:21:16,744
Inside one budget, the arms also compete for spend.

977
01:21:17,024 --> 01:21:20,373
So add a campaign-level design and long-run holdouts.

978
01:21:20,653 --> 01:21:25,261
Report results by category and advertiser size, with confidence intervals.

979
01:21:25,541 --> 01:21:27,995
Chapter four covers these auction mechanics.

980
01:21:29,435 --> 01:21:34,811
The hard part of this role is not auction mechanics, which I can learn and partly already have.

981
01:21:35,091 --> 01:21:41,897
It is building product-faithful generative systems, and evaluating them reliably when models judge model output.

982
01:21:42,177 --> 01:21:48,235
I built a reference-free quality metric, published how it was gamed, and shipped a system that beat Google's.

983
01:21:48,515 --> 01:21:50,179
Follow with one ads insight.

984
01:21:51,609 --> 01:21:52,953
Now the coding drills.

985
01:21:53,233 --> 01:21:58,609
The creative drills file has fourteen problems, using only numpy, each with self-tests.

986
01:21:58,889 --> 01:22:10,281
First, judge statistics: Bradley Terry from pairwise preferences, Cohen's kappa, isotonic calibration with an auto-approve threshold, and position bias in a pairwise judge.

987
01:22:10,561 --> 01:22:19,116
Next, monitoring: P-S-I and KS drift with an alert rule, plus CUSUM, a cumulative sum detector on daily approval rate.

988
01:22:19,396 --> 01:22:23,471
Then composition. wuh-cag, the web accessibility contrast check.

989
01:22:23,751 --> 01:22:26,887
A saliency smart crop using a summed-area table.

990
01:22:27,167 --> 01:22:31,711
Headline placement as the largest calm rectangle, and a layout validator.

991
01:22:31,991 --> 01:22:39,372
Then fidelity: S-sim, the structural similarity index, perceptual hashes, and delta E in C-I-E lab color.

992
01:22:39,652 --> 01:22:42,596
Last, Thompson sampling and near-duplicate dedupe.

993
01:22:42,876 --> 01:22:50,407
The older ads file adds A-U-C, calibration, the GSP or generalized second price auction, and pacing.

994
01:22:51,836 --> 01:22:53,266
Here is how to practice.

995
01:22:53,546 --> 01:22:57,962
Read one drill's docstring, which is the interviewer's prompt, and close the file.

996
01:22:58,242 --> 01:23:01,570
Implement it in ten to fifteen minutes while talking out loud.

997
01:23:01,850 --> 01:23:09,615
Restate the problem, give the brute force, then the better algorithm, its complexity, and edge cases like ties and empty input.

998
01:23:09,895 --> 01:23:13,650
Run the self-tests, and have one sentence on how it runs at scale.

999
01:23:13,930 --> 01:23:15,530
Now the five priorities.

1000
01:23:15,810 --> 01:23:21,100
First, isotonic calibration with the auto-approve threshold, literally the judge gate.

1001
01:23:21,380 --> 01:23:24,090
Second, Cohen's kappa against the human ceiling.

1002
01:23:24,370 --> 01:23:27,570
Third, Bradley Terry, for pairwise model selection.

1003
01:23:27,850 --> 01:23:33,610
Fourth, P-S-I and KS drift, where a tiny p-value means nothing without an effect size.

1004
01:23:33,890 --> 01:23:38,263
Fifth, the wuh-cag contrast ratio, short and very creative-specific.

1005
01:23:40,061 --> 01:23:42,643
This last chapter turns everything into a plan.

1006
01:23:42,923 --> 01:23:45,781
We will lay out Friday through Monday, block by block.

1007
01:23:46,061 --> 01:23:51,416
Then come the Monday morning essentials, your questions for them, the logistics, and how to close.

1008
01:23:51,696 --> 01:23:53,467
Then a word on why you are ready.

1009
01:23:55,096 --> 01:23:59,363
Today is Friday, and the order matters, because the riskiest gap goes first.

1010
01:23:59,643 --> 01:24:01,883
Friday morning is the judge chapter.

1011
01:24:02,163 --> 01:24:05,448
Read it fully, take notes, and run drills one through six.

1012
01:24:05,728 --> 01:24:11,702
Friday afternoon is generative image, copy and composition, with drills seven through twelve.

1013
01:24:11,982 --> 01:24:15,310
Saturday morning covers video and creative understanding.

1014
01:24:15,590 --> 01:24:20,880
Saturday afternoon, say system designs one and two out loud with a forty-minute timer.

1015
01:24:21,160 --> 01:24:25,704
Sunday morning, go through every diagram until you can draw four from memory.

1016
01:24:25,984 --> 01:24:30,955
Sunday afternoon, answer the starred questions and your behavioral stories aloud.

1017
01:24:31,235 --> 01:24:38,062
Sunday evening, run a full mock: pitch, a system design, a judge deep dive, two stories, and your questions.

1018
01:24:38,342 --> 01:24:41,371
Monday morning, read only the cheat sheet, then rest.

1019
01:24:41,651 --> 01:24:44,616
Fill in your real story numbers before Sunday's mock.

1020
01:24:46,055 --> 01:24:48,700
On Monday morning, hold five things in your head.

1021
01:24:48,980 --> 01:24:55,231
First, the role in one line: generate, judge, understand, adapt and learn, on shared infrastructure.

1022
01:24:55,511 --> 01:25:00,097
Second, be able to draw the judge stack from deterministic checks to the router.

1023
01:25:00,377 --> 01:25:09,017
Third, your formulas: Cohen's kappa for agreement, P-S-I for drift, E-C-E for calibration error, and the wuh-cag contrast ratio.

1024
01:25:09,297 --> 01:25:12,967
Add eCPM, effective cost per thousand impressions.

1025
01:25:13,247 --> 01:25:20,052
It equals bid times predicted CTR times one thousand, which is why creative quality moves revenue.

1026
01:25:20,332 --> 01:25:24,641
Fourth, the Goodhart line: separate the gatekeeper, the coach and the scoreboard.

1027
01:25:24,921 --> 01:25:30,169
Fifth, if you get stuck, say: let me separate what must be guaranteed from what should be optimized.

1028
01:25:31,599 --> 01:25:33,519
Pick three or four questions to ask.

1029
01:25:33,799 --> 01:25:39,537
Where is the bottleneck today: generation quality, review throughput, or advertiser adoption?

1030
01:25:39,817 --> 01:25:44,937
How is generated creative evaluated now, and how much still goes through human review?

1031
01:25:45,217 --> 01:25:49,356
Are long-tail sellers without design resources the primary customer?

1032
01:25:49,636 --> 01:25:52,687
How do creative signals reach ranking and targeting?

1033
01:25:52,967 --> 01:25:55,804
And what does success look like for this role in twelve months?

1034
01:25:56,084 --> 01:26:02,121
Each one shows you think in systems and outcomes, and the answers tell you where your first ninety days should start.

1035
01:26:02,401 --> 01:26:06,881
For logistics, join five minutes early with water and paper for diagrams.

1036
01:26:07,161 --> 01:26:12,815
Ask clarifying questions before you design, and end every design with risks and a roadmap.

1037
01:26:14,265 --> 01:26:22,052
The overlap between what this role needs and what I've built, faithful generation and honest evaluation, is unusually high.

1038
01:26:22,332 --> 01:26:24,273
I'd love to build this platform here.

1039
01:26:24,553 --> 01:26:26,260
Use it at the end, and mean it.

1040
01:26:26,540 --> 01:26:30,359
Your record backs it: SpaceWarp beat Google and shipped as default.

1041
01:26:30,639 --> 01:26:33,647
You published a judge and its honest negative result.

1042
01:26:33,927 --> 01:26:37,681
Seven publications, five patents, four E-V-P awards.

1043
01:26:37,961 --> 01:26:38,857
You are ready.

1044
01:26:39,137 --> 01:26:40,161
Good luck, Aamer.
